#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-onboarding-derivation -- the operator onboarding guide published on
# kiosk.tech teaches a worked verb whose ORIGINAL lives in this repository, and
# until this script nothing compared the two (K-1324, K-1325, K-1326;
# extended to the WRITE verbs by K-1338 and K-1339).
#
# THE DEFECT, measured rather than argued. `kiosk.tech/onboarding.html` is the
# page an operator follows from `bundle install` to a served wire. It carries a
# full controller pair as copyable Ruby, and every line of it is a copy of
# something here: the class names are the ones the `kiosk:install` generator
# writes into an initializer as its worked example, and the domain -- catalogue,
# delivery windows, settlement-gated reschedule -- is kiosk-demo-getgrocery's.
#
# On 2026-09-06 three drifts were found there by a human reading the page:
#
#   * getgrocery's `delivery_slots` had made `date` OPTIONAL and
#     `delivery_address` REQUIRED, for a reason its own code states -- the
#     caller cannot compute the operator's today. The page still taught
#     `required: ["date"]`, called `params.require(:date)`, had no
#     `delivery_address` at all, and froze a calendar literal into
#     `example_params` -- a date already in the past, which the live verb
#     refuses. The drift landed in `ede32f70` and was found TEN HOURS later.
#   * The page taught `params[:delivery_slot_id].to_i`, which turns `"1.5"`
#     into slot 1 inside the declared 1..6 range. Both demo origins refuse it,
#     and one of them names the idiom outright.
#   * The page said getgrocery's handlers were the classes it shows. They are
#     not: getgrocery registers `Kiosk::StorefrontController`, the page shows
#     `Kiosk::CatalogController`, and `git grep -n "CatalogController"` over
#     this repository answers with the e2e fixture, this gem's README, the
#     install-generator template and specs -- none of them getgrocery.
#
# Two more were found one verb over on 2026-09-06, and they are why the rules
# below are no longer scoped to a single named verb:
#
#   * The page REQUIRED `delivery_date` on both write verbs. getgrocery
#     requires it on neither -- omitting it books tomorrow, for the same reason
#     `delivery_slots` leaves its own `date` optional: the caller cannot compute
#     the operator's today. So the page taught a stricter contract than the
#     origin it was copied from (K-1338).
#   * The page's `create_order` indexed every cart entry without checking it is
#     a Hash, so `items: ["bread"]` -- a well-formed request with the wrong
#     shape -- raised inside the handler and reached the caller as a 500 about
#     the operator's server, where getgrocery's own guard answers a typed 400
#     naming the shape it wanted (K-1339).
#
# The first of those also found a bug in this script rather than only on the
# page: `required_list` returned the FIRST `required:` in a verb's region, which
# for `create_order` is the ITEM schema's `required: ["sku", "qty"]`, nested two
# braces down. Both sides answered with that nested list, so the comparison was
# two copies of one irrelevant fact and passed on a page that required an
# argument getgrocery leaves optional. `top_level_required` walks brace depth
# instead; the plant that exposed it is a self-test arm.
#
# NOTHING HELD ANY OF IT, IN EITHER REPOSITORY. `bin/check-demo-derivations`
# compares each demo's `before-after.md` against THAT demo's own producers and
# its file set is this repository; `kiosk.tech/bin/check-derived-counts` holds
# typed cardinals, not code blocks, and its own header records why it did not
# reach across the boundary for one. So the guide could drift from its source
# indefinitely, and it did -- onto a published page.
#
# ── WHY IT LIVES HERE, AND WHAT THAT COSTS ──────────────────────────────────
#
# The comparison needs a file from each repository, and neither CI has the
# other checked out. MEASURED at head 2026-09-06 with `command grep -c
# "repository:"`: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` here -> 0, and the five workflow
# files in the sibling -> 0 between them. So a checkout-based reach would run
# on a developer's machine and silently no-op in every CI job, which is the
# "reads as coverage, holds nothing" shape this workspace keeps re-discovering.
# `check-derived-counts` weighed exactly this and declined it for a cardinal.
#
# What it did not weigh is the OTHER reach, and this repository already uses
# it: `bin/check-solver-pin --network` fetches what kiosk.tech actually serves,
# because a commit in that repository can break a pin here without triggering
# this workflow. The direction is INVERTED for the onboarding page -- a commit
# HERE breaks a page THERE -- so this script belongs on this side, where the
# drift is authored, next to the derivation check whose boundary it extends.
#
# THE PAGE IS RESOLVED IN A STATED ORDER, AND THE ORDER IS PRINTED:
#
#   1. `--page PATH`                 -- an explicit file.
#   2. `--network`                   -- https://kiosk.tech/onboarding.html, what
#      an operator is actually reading. 3 attempts with backoff, so a blip does
#      not read as drift.
#   3. `$KIOSK_TECH_DIR/onboarding.html`
#   4. `../kiosk.tech/onboarding.html` -- the sibling checkout, which is what a
#      workspace session and the convergence loop have. Tree against tree: this
#      mode is never spuriously red.
#
# THE TWO FLAGS OUTRANK THE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, AND `--network` DID NOT USED
# TO (K-1388). It was resolved after `$KIOSK_TECH_DIR`, so setting that variable
# silently turned the network mode into a local read while the run line claimed
# a checkout. It now wins, and when it overrides a set variable the provenance
# line SAYS the variable was ignored -- an override a reader cannot see is the
# same defect one step quieter. `--page` together with `--network` is refused
# outright: they are two explicit selectors, and picking one is guessing.
#
# IF NONE OF THEM RESOLVES, THIS EXITS 1 AND SAYS THE CHECK DID NOT RUN. It
# does not skip. A guard that quietly no-ops when its subject is absent is
# indistinguishable from a guard that passed, and that is the failure this
# script exists to close rather than to commit.
#
# THE COST OF `--network`, stated so nobody is surprised by it: between a
# commit here that moves a verb and the sibling's deploy of the matching page,
# it is CORRECTLY red. That is why it is not on `push` -- it would gate every
# merge in this repository on a third-party fetch, which is the trade
# `check-solver-pin` already declined in writing -- and why the CI job runs it
# on the daily schedule, where a red default branch notifies, exactly as
# `solver-pin-live` does. The `--self-test` arm has no such dependency and DOES
# run on every push, because a rule that has stopped matching anything is the
# other way this goes quietly green.
#
# ── WHAT A RULE IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A rule DERIVES a fact from this repository and holds the page to it. Nothing
# below is ever compared to a second hand-typed copy of the same fact, because
# that moves the defect rather than closing it. Two kinds:
#
#   DERIVATION  the page must AGREE with a value read out of this repository.
#   BAN         the page must not contain a construct this repository refuses.
#               A ban's subject list is derived too, so it cannot rot into a
#               list of names nobody maintains.
#
# THE VACUITY ARM. Every rule declares what it needs to find in order to be
# holding anything at all -- a derivation that computes, a live site in the
# page, a non-empty derived subject list. A rule that finds none of that FAILS
# and names itself, rather than passing. That is not decoration: one repository
# over, a relink rule required a word that no live sentence used, so it matched
# nothing for its entire existence and nothing said so.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT CATCH, so nothing here is overtrusted:
#   * Prose, WITH ONE SUB-CLASS SUBTRACTED (K-1519). The page's sentences about
#     the verb -- what it does, why an argument is optional, what a caller
#     should do next -- are derived from nothing. Its STATUS-BESIDE-A-CODE
#     sentences are: `kiosk.tech/bin/check-problem-pages` holds every one of
#     them, on either side of the repository boundary, against
#     `spec/schemas/problem.schema.json`. That bullet used to make the wider
#     claim, and K-1501 quoted it as the evidence that the class was covered by
#     nothing -- which it was, until K-1501's own fix. Neither script can see
#     the other, so this clause is CHECKED and not trusted: rule STATUS_PROSE
#     below looks the named guard up in the sibling checkout and says so when it
#     cannot.
#   * A verb the page teaches that has no counterpart here at all.
#   * The page agreeing with a getgrocery that is itself wrong. Consistency,
#     never truth.
#   * Whether the FLOOR is right. RAILS_FLOOR and RUBY_FLOOR hold the page to
#     what the gemspecs DECLARE. Whether the declaration is itself honest is a
#     different question, answered by the CI leg that runs the suites on the
#     declared Ruby floor -- not by this script.
#   * Whether the SERVED page equals the sibling's working tree. `--network`
#     answers about the served bytes and the default mode about the tree; they
#     are two questions and the run says which one it asked.
#
# Stdlib only: no bundle, no services, no database. It reads files, and reaches
# the network only under `--network`.
#
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation             # sibling checkout
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --network   # the published page
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --list      # every rule and what it derives
#   bin/check-onboarding-derivation --self-test # red on a plant of every rule
#
# CI ARM: bin/check-onboarding-derivation --network

require "net/http"
require "uri"
require "set"
# Only `--self-test` writes anything: the K-1388 precedence arm needs a decoy
# checkout on disk, because that is the only way to ask whether `--network`
# really outranks `KIOSK_TECH_DIR`. An ordinary run touches neither.
require "tmpdir"
require "fileutils"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# The three files in THIS repository every rule below derives from.
GENERATOR_INITIALIZER = "kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/templates/initializer.rb.tt"
GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/controllers/kiosk/storefront_controller.rb"
GETGROCERY_ORDERS     = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/controllers/kiosk/orders_controller.rb"
GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/config/initializers/kiosk.rb"
# The two files that hold the Ruby CONSTANTS getgrocery writes INTO its
# `input_schema` declarations -- `Kiosk::UuidCheck::JSON_SCHEMA_PATTERN` and
# `WireArguments::MAX_INT4`. R14 used to count those keywords and print them
# because the page's standalone controller can name no constant of the demo's;
# it now RESOLVES them to the literal each one is assigned and asserts against
# that, so a page that inlines the literal is held to it and a page that omits
# it is red. A constant it cannot resolve is an error, never a pass (K-1354).
# The uuid shape is no longer getgrocery's: T-189 promoted it into kiosk-core,
# which is why one of these two paths leaves the demo tree.
CORE_UUID_CHECK           = "kiosk-core/lib/kiosk/uuid_check.rb"
GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/operations/wire_arguments.rb"
# The origin's create verb, and the only file that says WHICH guard the summed
# cart total is handed to. R15 walks it: the local the sum is bound to, the
# module function that local goes into, the constant that function bounds it
# against, and the literal that constant is assigned (K-1359).
GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/operations/create_order_operation.rb"
# R16's two extra sources. The demo's delivery-window clock is the thing BOTH
# write verbs refuse a stale window against, and the operations DIRECTORY is
# read as a directory rather than as a list of names, so a verb that gains an
# Operation is picked up instead of being silently uncovered (K-1366).
GETGROCERY_DELIVERY_SLOTS = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/models/delivery_slots.rb"
GETGROCERY_OPERATIONS_DIR = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/app/operations"
# THE ROUTES, WHICH THE PAGE NOW TEACHES LINE BY LINE (T-184). Until T-183 the
# engine drew one constrained `:kiosk_verb` pair and the page could honestly say
# «mounting it draws the entire surface -- you write no route of your own». That
# pair is gone: the mount draws the PROTOCOL PLANE and the operator writes ONE
# EXPLICIT LINE PER VERB. A page teaching that has to carry a worked routes file,
# and a hand-typed one is exactly the drift this script exists to refuse -- so
# both halves are derived from the origin the rest of the page is copied from.
# `config/routes.rb` for the `draw` call that reaches the split, and
# `config/routes/kiosk.rb` for the mount line and every verb route below it.
GETGROCERY_APP_ROUTES  = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/config/routes.rb"
GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES = "kiosk-demo-getgrocery/config/routes/kiosk.rb"
# The engine's gemspec is the ONLY one that names Rails at all, so it is the
# single authority for the floor an adopting operator has to clear (K-1320).
SERVER_GEMSPEC = "kiosk-server/kiosk-server.gemspec"
# kiosk-core's `required_ruby_version` is the floor every gemspec declares, and
# is the same source `bin/check-demo-prereqs` derives its Ruby line from -- so
# the page and the eight demo READMEs cannot drift apart (K-1321).
CORE_GEMSPEC = "kiosk-core/kiosk-core.gemspec"
# Where publication status and the canonical install form are stated
# CANONICALLY (K-1319). The page points at this section AND carries a derived
# copy of its claim, which R17 holds to these bytes -- the earlier wording here
# and in the README said this was the ONE PLACE either published repository
# states publication status while the page stated it three lines above its own
# pointer at this file, so the claim was false the day it was written (K-1381).
MONOREPO_README = "README.md"
# The rescue seam the page teaches under «raise what you would raise anyway»,
# and the file holding the sentences it renders INSTEAD of the exception's own
# (K-1341). Two files because the two halves of that fact live apart: the mixin
# decides WHETHER the message travels, `errors.rb` decides WHAT does.
SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN = "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/handler_mixin.rb"
SERVER_ERRORS        = "kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/errors.rb"

PAGE_URL      = "https://kiosk.tech/onboarding.html"
PAGE_BASENAME = "onboarding.html"

# ── The one sub-class of the page's PROSE that IS derived (K-1519) ───────────
#
# The disclaimer list above opens «Prose. The page's sentences about the verb
# are not derived from anything», and K-1501 quoted that bullet as the evidence
# that a status written into a sentence was «known, written down, and covered by
# nothing». It stopped being true the day K-1501 was fixed — one repository over,
# `kiosk.tech/bin/check-problem-pages` scans every tracked page in the site
# repository for a three-digit status ADJACENT to a vocabulary code and holds
# each pair to `spec/schemas/problem.schema.json`, and `onboarding.html` is in
# that file set. It exists because this page published `403` for two
# `module_not_served` refusals while all three tables on the same site published
# `501`.
#
# So a reader of THIS script, deciding whether their edit to that page's status
# prose is held, was being told «nothing derives it» at the moment the answer
# became «one guard in the sibling repository does». Neither script can see the
# other, which is exactly why the clause below is CHECKED rather than trusted:
# a pointer to a mechanism in another repository is the one kind of sentence
# that can go false without anything in THIS repository changing at all.
SITE_PROBLEM_GUARD = "bin/check-problem-pages"

# The code vocabulary, read out of the schema mirror this repository already
# carries — not typed here. `bin/check-spec-schemas` holds that mirror
# byte-identical to the one kiosk.tech publishes and the sibling guard reads, so
# the two sides cannot disagree about what a code is.
PROBLEM_SCHEMA_MIRROR = "e2e/schemas/problem.schema.json"

# [[code, status], …] out of the schema's own `if`/`then` branches — the same
# binding the sibling guard reads, in the same file, so neither side can be
# holding a vocabulary the other does not have.
def problem_pairs(raw)
  require "json"
  branches = JSON.parse(raw)["allOf"]
  return [] unless branches.is_a?(Array)

  branches.filter_map do |b|
    code   = b.dig("if", "properties", "code", "const")
    status = b.dig("then", "properties", "status", "const")
    next if code.nil? || status.nil?

    [code, status]
  end
rescue StandardError
  []
end

def problem_codes(raw) = problem_pairs(raw).map(&:first).uniq

# A status and a code within a short run of non-alphanumeric characters, in
# either order — the same shape the sibling guard scans for. This side only
# needs to know the page HAS such sentences: whether each one is RIGHT is that
# guard's answer, and duplicating it here would be a second opinion nobody
# reconciles.
def status_code_pairs(page, codes)
  return 0 if codes.empty?

  alt = codes.map { |c| Regexp.escape(c) }.join("|")
  re  = /(?:\b[1-5]\d\d\b[^A-Za-z0-9]{0,16}(?:#{alt})\b)|(?:\b(?:#{alt})\b[^A-Za-z0-9]{0,16}[1-5]\d\d\b)/
  page.scan(re).size
end

# The sibling checkout, when there is one. `resolve_page` already knows three
# ways to reach the PAGE; this asks the narrower question of whether the
# sibling's `bin/` is on this disk, which only the checkout branch can answer.
# `KIOSK_TECH_DIR` wins for the same reason it does there — it names the site
# checkout — and it is also what makes this leg PLANTABLE: an arm can point it
# at a directory with no `bin/` and watch the rule go red, which is the only way
# to prove a filesystem lookup is really being made.
def site_checkout
  env = ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"]
  return (File.directory?(env) ? env : nil) if env && !env.empty?

  dir = File.expand_path("../kiosk.tech", ROOT)
  File.directory?(dir) ? dir : nil
end

# The verb both sides declare, and the one K-1325's drift was in.
WORKED_VERB = "delivery_slots"

# ── Reading the sources ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def repo_read(rel)
  path = File.join(ROOT, rel)
  raise "derivation source missing: #{rel}" unless File.file?(path)

  File.read(path)
end

# Every `.rb` under one directory, keyed by basename. A DIRECTORY and not a
# list of names: R16 resolves «which Operation does this verb hand its
# arguments to» out of the controller, and a hand-kept file list would go stale
# the moment a verb gained one -- silently, which is the shape this whole
# script exists to refuse.
def repo_read_dir(rel)
  dir = File.join(ROOT, rel)
  raise "derivation source directory missing: #{rel}" unless File.directory?(dir)

  Dir[File.join(dir, "*.rb")].sort.to_h { |p| [File.basename(p), File.read(p)] }
end

# A FETCHED BODY IS BYTES, AND EVERY RULE BELOW COMPARES IT AS TEXT (K-1367).
#
# `Net::HTTP` hands back a body tagged ASCII-8BIT — it read a socket, and no
# amount of `Content-Type: charset=utf-8` changes what Ruby tags the String
# with. A file read off disk comes back UTF-8, so the file-backed modes never
# met this; `--network` did, on EVERY run, and the first rule whose needle
# carries a non-ASCII byte raised `Encoding::CompatibilityError` out of
# `String#include?`. Unrescued, it escaped `run_rules` at rule 13 of 15, so the
# three newest rules had never once been evaluated against the published page
# and no summary was printed. It cannot manufacture a green — an unhandled
# exception exits 1 — but a check that aborts two rules short while its own
# output claims fifteen is not the check anybody thinks is running.
#
# So the body is decoded HERE, once, at the seam that produces it: the declared
# charset when the response names one it knows, UTF-8 otherwise, and `scrub`
# for bytes that are neither, because a page served with a broken byte is a
# fact about the page and must reach the rules as text rather than as a crash.
def decoded_body(res)
  declared = begin
    res.type_params["charset"]
  rescue StandardError
    nil
  end
  enc = begin
    declared ? Encoding.find(declared) : Encoding::UTF_8
  rescue ArgumentError
    Encoding::UTF_8
  end
  text = res.body.to_s.dup.force_encoding(enc)
  text = text.encode(Encoding::UTF_8, invalid: :replace, undef: :replace) unless enc == Encoding::UTF_8
  text.valid_encoding? ? text : text.scrub
end

# GET with retries. A transient blip must not read as drift, so "could not
# reach it" and "reached it and the bytes are wrong" stay different answers.
def fetch(url, attempts: 3)
  last = nil
  attempts.times do |i|
    sleep(2**i) if i.positive?
    begin
      res = Net::HTTP.get_response(URI(url))
      return [decoded_body(res), nil] if res.is_a?(Net::HTTPOK)

      last = "HTTP #{res.code}"
    rescue StandardError => e
      last = "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
    end
  end
  [nil, "unreachable after #{attempts} attempts (#{last})"]
end

# The page, plus a sentence saying WHERE it came from. Never a silent skip:
# every branch either returns bytes with a provenance or returns the reason
# there are none, and the caller exits 1 on the second.
# ── `--network` MEANS THE NETWORK, AND AN OVERRIDE IS NEVER SILENT (K-1388) ──
#
# The env var used to be read BEFORE this branch, so
# `KIOSK_TECH_DIR=... bin/check-onboarding-derivation --network` handed back a
# LOCAL CHECKOUT -- or exited 1 saying that local file was missing -- while the
# operator had asked for the published page and the run line said a checkout had
# been read. Clean exit code either way. That is the "answered a different
# question than the one asked" shape, and it is worse than a plain bug because
# the flag reads as a MODE SELECTOR: the whole reason to type it is to find out
# what kiosk.tech is actually serving.
#
# So the precedence is the ordinary one -- an explicit FLAG beats ambient
# configuration -- and the shadowing is SAID OUT LOUD in the provenance line
# rather than left to be inferred. The one case that is not a precedence
# question at all is `--page` WITH `--network`: two explicit selectors naming
# two different sources, where preferring either silently is guessing. That is
# refused by name.
def resolve_page(explicit:, network:)
  if explicit && network
    return [nil, nil,
            "--page #{explicit} and --network name two different sources and this script will not " \
            "choose between them: one asks for a file on this disk, the other for the bytes " \
            "kiosk.tech is serving. Drop one (K-1388)."]
  end

  if explicit
    return [nil, nil, "--page #{explicit} does not exist"] unless File.file?(explicit)

    return [File.read(explicit), "--page #{explicit}", nil]
  end

  env_dir = ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"]

  if network
    body, err = fetch(PAGE_URL)
    return [nil, nil, "#{PAGE_URL}: #{err}"] if body.nil?

    shadowed = (env_dir && !env_dir.empty?) ? " -- KIOSK_TECH_DIR=#{env_dir} IGNORED, --network was asked for" : ""
    return [body, "the published page #{PAGE_URL} (#{body.bytesize} bytes)#{shadowed}", nil]
  end

  if env_dir && !env_dir.empty?
    path = File.join(env_dir, PAGE_BASENAME)
    return [nil, nil, "KIOSK_TECH_DIR is set but #{path} does not exist"] unless File.file?(path)

    return [File.read(path), "KIOSK_TECH_DIR #{path}", nil]
  end

  sibling = File.expand_path("../kiosk.tech/#{PAGE_BASENAME}", ROOT)
  return [File.read(sibling), "sibling checkout #{sibling}", nil] if File.file?(sibling)

  [nil, nil,
   "no sibling checkout at #{sibling}, no KIOSK_TECH_DIR, no --network. " \
   "THIS CHECK DID NOT RUN -- it is not a pass."]
end

# ── Ruby-source parsing, shared by both sides ────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything below is a static read of declaration text. It never evaluates
# anything, and it is deliberately narrow: it looks only at the constructs both
# sides actually use, and a construct it does not recognise makes the rule that
# needed it VACUOUS (and therefore red) rather than absent.

# Strip Ruby comments and the contents of double-quoted strings, so a `{` in a
# description sentence cannot be mistaken for a hash brace. Bytes are replaced
# rather than removed, so every index still lines up with the original.
# Memoised: `masked` is a pure function of its input, and the self-test runs
# every rule once per arm — thirty-odd times over the same handful of sources.
# Without this the char-by-char walk below is re-run several hundred times and
# the arm suite takes minutes instead of seconds.
MASK_CACHE = {}

def masked(src)
  cached = MASK_CACHE[src]
  return cached if cached

  MASK_CACHE[src] = masked!(src)
end

def masked!(src)
  out    = src.dup
  i      = 0
  len    = out.length
  in_str = false
  while i < len
    c = out[i]
    if in_str
      if c == "\\"
        out[i] = " "
        out[i + 1] = " " if i + 1 < len && out[i + 1] != "\n"
        i += 2
        next
      end
      if c == '"'
        in_str = false
      elsif c != "\n"
        out[i] = " "
      end
    elsif c == '"'
      in_str = true
    elsif c == "#"
      # A comment runs to end of line. Blank it, newline kept so line numbers
      # and every later index still line up with the original.
      while i < len && out[i] != "\n"
        out[i] = " "
        i += 1
      end
      next
    end
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

# STRUCTURE IS FOUND ON THE MASKED COPY, VALUES ARE READ OFF THE ORIGINAL.
# `masked` preserves every byte position, so a range located in one indexes the
# other. Reading a value off the masked copy is how the first writing of this
# script derived `required: []` from a line that plainly says
# `required: ["delivery_address"]` -- the quoted names had been blanked, both
# sides came back empty, and the rule compared two nothings and passed.
#
# The declaration text a `def <name>` claims: from the `kind :` macro that
# opens it up to the `def` line itself, as a Range.
def declaration_range(mask, verb)
  at = mask.index(/^\s*def #{Regexp.escape(verb)}\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  kinds = mask[0...at].enum_for(:scan, /^\s*kind\s+:/).map { Regexp.last_match.begin(0) }
  return nil if kinds.empty?

  (kinds.last...at)
end

# The `input_schema ...` part of a declaration -- up to whatever macro comes
# next, so `output_schema`'s own `required:` can never be read as the input's.
def input_schema_range(mask, decl_range)
  region = mask[decl_range]
  from   = region.index(/^\s*input_schema\b/)
  return nil if from.nil?

  stop = region.index(/^\s*(?:output_schema|example_params|example_row|reach|def)\b/, from + 1)
  base = decl_range.first + from
  (base...(stop ? decl_range.first + stop : decl_range.last))
end

# Every `input_schema ...` region in a whole source, as Ranges.
def input_schema_ranges(mask)
  ranges = []
  mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*input_schema\b/).each do
    from = Regexp.last_match.begin(0)
    stop = mask.index(/^\s*(?:output_schema|example_params|example_row|reach|def)\b/, from + 1)
    ranges << (from...(stop || mask.length))
  end
  ranges
end

# `required: ["a", "b"]` or `required: %w[a b]`, as a list of names -- the one
# at the TOP LEVEL of the slice, never a nested one.
#
# THE DEPTH IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND THE FIRST WRITING OF THIS FUNCTION DID NOT
# HAVE IT. A plain "first `required:` in the region" reads
# `create_order`'s ITEM schema -- `required: ["sku", "qty"]`, nested two braces
# down inside `items:` -- and never reaches the argument list the verb actually
# requires. Both sides answered `["sku", "qty"]`, so a rule comparing them
# compared two copies of the same nested fact and passed on a page that
# required an argument getgrocery leaves optional. That is the identical shape
# this file's own header records about reading values off the masked copy: two
# nothings, equal, green.
#
# Structure is walked on the MASKED copy (a bracket inside a description
# sentence is not depth) and the literal is read off the ORIGINAL at the same
# index (the names live inside string literals, which the mask blanks).
def top_level_required(mask_slice, src_slice)
  depth = 0
  i     = 0
  while i < mask_slice.length
    c = mask_slice[i]
    if ["{", "["].include?(c)
      depth += 1
    elsif ["}", "]"].include?(c)
      depth -= 1
    elsif depth.zero? && mask_slice[i, 9] == "required:"
      m = src_slice[i..].match(/\Arequired:\s*(\[[^\]]*\]|%w\[[^\]]*\])/m)
      return m[1].scan(/[a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*/) if m
    end
    i += 1
  end
  nil
end

# The property names declared at the TOP level of a `properties: {...}` hash.
def property_names(slice)
  at = slice.index(/properties:\s*\{/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  open  = slice.index("{", at)
  depth = 0
  names = []
  i     = open
  while i < slice.length
    c = slice[i]
    if c == "{"
      depth += 1
    elsif c == "}"
      depth -= 1
      break if depth.zero?
    elsif depth == 1 && (m = slice[i..].match(/\A([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{/)) &&
          (i.zero? || slice[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/)
      names << m[1]
    end
    i += 1
  end
  names
end

# The top-level entries of a `properties: {...}` hash as {name => Range}, where
# the Range spans that property's own brace-balanced body. Structure is walked
# on the MASKED copy and the range indexes the ORIGINAL, which is the same
# discipline `verb_input_schema` uses: a `{` inside a description sentence is
# not structure, and a `type: "array"` inside a string literal is not readable
# from the mask.
def property_body_ranges(mask, base)
  slice = mask[base]
  at    = slice.index(/properties:\s*\{/)
  return {} if at.nil?

  open  = slice.index("{", at)
  depth = 0
  out   = {}
  i     = open
  while i < slice.length
    c = slice[i]
    if c == "{"
      depth += 1
    elsif c == "}"
      depth -= 1
      break if depth.zero?
    elsif depth == 1 && (m = slice[i..].match(/\A([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{/)) &&
          (i.zero? || slice[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/)
      bstart = i + m[0].length - 1
      d      = 0
      j      = bstart
      while j < slice.length
        d += 1 if slice[j] == "{"
        if slice[j] == "}"
          d -= 1
          break if d.zero?
        end
        j += 1
      end
      out[m[1]] = ((base.first + bstart)..(base.first + j))
    end
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

# Every INPUT parameter a source declares as an ARRAY OF OBJECTS -- the subject
# list of the element-shape ban, derived rather than typed. A declaration whose
# own body says both `type: "array"` and `type: "object"` is an array whose
# elements are objects, which is exactly the shape a handler may not index
# without checking (K-1339).
def array_of_object_param_names(src)
  names = Set.new
  mask  = masked(src)
  input_schema_ranges(mask).each do |range|
    property_body_ranges(mask, range).each do |name, body|
      text = src[body]
      names << name if text.include?('type: "array"') && text.include?('type: "object"')
    end
  end
  names
end

# Every VERB a source declares: the mixin's contract is that each class-level
# `kind :` macro is claimed by the NEXT `def`, so that pairing is the whole
# rule. A `def` that no macro reaches -- a private helper, a plain module
# function -- is not a verb and is not returned, which is why this cannot be
# replaced by "every def in the file".
def declared_verbs(src)
  mask  = masked(src)
  kinds = mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*kind\s+:/).map { Regexp.last_match.begin(0) }
  defs  = mask.enum_for(:scan, /^\s*def ([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)/)
              .map { [Regexp.last_match.begin(0), Regexp.last_match(1)] }
  kinds.filter_map { |k| defs.find { |(at, _)| at > k }&.last }.uniq
end

# The BODY a `def <verb>` opens, as a Range: from the def line to whatever ends
# the declaration -- the next `kind :` macro, or the `private` keyword that
# opens the helper section. Scoping a ban to one verb's body is what keeps
# "this argument is optional HERE" from being read against a verb that requires
# the same name.
def handler_body_range(mask, verb)
  at = mask.index(/^\s*def #{Regexp.escape(verb)}\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  stop = mask.index(/^\s*(?:kind\s+:|private\s*$)/, at + 1)
  (at...(stop || mask.length))
end

# Every parameter name declared `type: "integer"` in a source's INPUT schemas.
# The subject list of the coercion ban, derived rather than typed, so a rename
# here reddens the rule instead of emptying it. Input schemas only: an output
# `price_cents: { type: "integer" }` is a field the operator RENDERS, never an
# argument anything coerces, and the first writing of this rule swept four
# names in where two belong.
def integer_param_names(src)
  names = Set.new
  mask  = masked(src)
  input_schema_ranges(mask).each do |range|
    src[range].scan(/([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*):\s*\{[^{}]*type:\s*"integer"/m) { names << Regexp.last_match(1) }
  end
  names
end

# The page ships its Ruby inside `<pre><code class="language-ruby">` with five
# characters entity-escaped. Rules about CODE read this, not the raw HTML: a
# `-&gt;` lambda and an `&amp;&amp;` would otherwise parse as neither Ruby nor
# prose, and a sentence in the surrounding page would be searched as if it were
# a handler.
ENTITIES = { "&lt;" => "<", "&gt;" => ">", "&quot;" => '"', "&#39;" => "'", "&amp;" => "&" }.freeze

# Every `language-ruby` block on the page, decoded, IN ORDER. A block is the
# unit an operator copies -- the routes file the page teaches is ONE of them --
# so the route rules below can ask "which block is the wire file" instead of
# asking the concatenation, which cannot tell a block apart from its neighbour.
def page_ruby_blocks(page)
  page.scan(%r{<code class="language-ruby">(.*?)</code>}m)
      .flatten
      .map { |b| b.gsub(/&(?:lt|gt|quot|#39|amp);/) { |e| ENTITIES[e] } }
end

def page_ruby(page)
  page_ruby_blocks(page).join("\n")
end

# ── THE ROUTES FILE, READ THE SAME WAY ON BOTH SIDES (T-184) ────────────────
#
# The same two expressions `bin/check-verb-routes` reads an origin's own wire
# file with, so the page and the demo are parsed by one reader rather than by
# two that can disagree. Deliberately anchored at line start: a route named
# inside a COMMENT (getgrocery's wire file explains the deleted `POST
# /kiosk/query` pair in its header) begins with `#` and cannot match.
WIRE_ROUTE_LINE = /^[ \t]*(get|post|put|patch|delete|match)[ \t]+"([^"]+)"[ \t]*,[ \t]*to:[ \t]*"([^"]+)"([^\n]*)/
WIRE_MOUNT_LINE = /^[ \t]*mount[ \t]+Kiosk::Server::Engine[ \t]*=>/
# A dynamic path segment -- `:kiosk_verb` or `*splat`. This is the shape T-183
# deleted, and the one thing a worked routes file must never re-teach.
DYNAMIC_SEGMENT = /[:*][a-z_]/
# `draw(:kiosk)` -- Rails' own routes-file split, which is how the wire file is
# reached from `config/routes.rb`.
DRAW_CALL = /^[ \t]*draw\(:([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)\)/

# Every drawn route in a routes source, with the verb name its `defaults:`
# carries (nil when it carries none -- which is what the deleted catch-all
# looked like) and the offset it was drawn at, because ORDER is the property
# the mount rule holds.
def wire_routes(src)
  out = []
  src.enum_for(:scan, WIRE_ROUTE_LINE).each do
    m = Regexp.last_match
    out << { method: m[1], path: m[2], to: m[3],
             verb: m[4][/defaults:[ \t]*\{[ \t]*kiosk_verb:[ \t]*"([^"]+)"/, 1],
             at: m.begin(0) }
  end
  out
end

# The engine mount: where it is drawn, and the line itself with its whitespace
# squeezed -- so a page that mounts at a hard-coded "/kiosk" instead of
# `Kiosk.configuration.mount_path` compares unequal rather than merely present.
def wire_mount_at(src)
  src.match(WIRE_MOUNT_LINE)&.begin(0)
end

def wire_mount_line(src)
  src[/^[ \t]*mount[ \t]+Kiosk::Server::Engine[^\n]*/]&.strip&.gsub(/[ \t]+/, " ")
end

# getgrocery's own wire, keyed by verb: the line each verb is drawn with.
def demo_verb_routes(sources)
  wire_routes(sources[:wire_file]).each_with_object({}) do |r, h|
    h[r[:verb]] = { method: r[:method], path: r[:path], to: r[:to] } if r[:verb]
  end
end

# The block on the page that IS the routes file: the one carrying the mount.
def page_wire_block(page)
  page_ruby_blocks(page).find { |b| b.match?(WIRE_MOUNT_LINE) }
end

# The class names a `c.handlers = %w[...]` line registers, in order. Reads a
# commented example too, which is what the generator template carries.
def handler_names(src)
  src.scan(/c\.handlers\s*=\s*%w\[([^\]]*)\]/).map { |m| m[0].split }
end

# The Rails MAJOR.MINOR the engine's gemspec declares. Every Rails component it
# names must agree: a gemspec pinning railties at `~> 8.1` and activerecord at
# `~> 8.0` has no single floor to state, so this returns nil and the rule that
# needs it reports itself VACUOUS rather than picking one.
RAILS_COMPONENTS = %w[railties actionpack activerecord activesupport].freeze

def rails_floor(gemspec_src)
  found = RAILS_COMPONENTS.filter_map do |c|
    m = gemspec_src.match(/add_dependency\s+"#{c}"\s*,\s*"~>\s*(\d+\.\d+)"/)
    m && m[1]
  end
  return nil if found.empty?

  found.uniq.length == 1 ? found.first : nil
end

# The Ruby floor a gemspec declares, as it is written: `>= 3.2.0`.
def ruby_floor(gemspec_src)
  m = gemspec_src.match(/required_ruby_version\s*=\s*">=\s*(\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"/)
  m && m[1]
end

# The canonical install's SOURCE QUALIFIER, read off the meta-gem line in the
# monorepo README: ["github", "kiosk-hq/kiosk"]. When the gems are published and
# that line loses its qualifier this returns nil, and INSTALL_SOURCE reports
# itself vacuous -- which is the correct moment for a human to restate the rule,
# not a moment to pass quietly.
def canonical_install_source(readme_src)
  m = readme_src.match(/^gem\s+"kiosk-all"\s*,\s*(github|git|path|source):\s*"([^"]+)"/)
  m && [m[1], m[2]]
end

# Every Gemfile line in the page's Ruby that installs a Kiosk gem.
def page_gem_lines(page)
  page_ruby(page).lines.select { |l| l.match?(/^\s*gem\s+"kiosk-[a-z0-9-]+"/) }
end

# ── The rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each returns [ok, description, detail]. `detail` is printed either way, so a
# green run is a measurement and not only an exit code.

FAILURES = []

def check(name, ok, what, detail)
  status = ok ? "ok  " : "FAIL"
  puts format("  %s %-22s %s", status, name, what)
  puts "         #{detail}" unless detail.to_s.empty?
  FAILURES << "#{name}: #{what} -- #{detail}" unless ok
  ok
end

def vacuous!(name, why)
  puts format("  %s %-22s VACUOUS -- holds nothing", "FAIL", name)
  puts "         #{why}"
  FAILURES << "#{name}: VACUOUS -- #{why}"
  false
end

# R0 -- the disclaimer list's own pointer, held rather than trusted (K-1519).
#
# It is a POINTER rather than a derivation and the RULES table says so: nothing
# here recomputes whether a status is RIGHT. That is the sibling guard's answer,
# and a second opinion in this repository would be one more thing to reconcile.
# What this holds is the two facts the clause rests on -- the page really does
# carry such sentences, and the guard the clause names really is there.
def rule_status_prose(page, sources)
  codes = problem_codes(sources[:problem_schema].to_s)
  if codes.empty?
    return vacuous!("STATUS_PROSE",
                    "#{PROBLEM_SCHEMA_MIRROR} yields no `const` code vocabulary, so this rule " \
                    "cannot tell a status-beside-a-code sentence from any other sentence and " \
                    "the disclaimer clause it holds is unverifiable")
  end

  pairs = status_code_pairs(page, codes)
  if pairs.zero?
    return vacuous!("STATUS_PROSE",
                    "the page states NO status beside a vocabulary code, so the disclaimer's " \
                    "«except its status-beside-a-code sentences» describes a sub-class this page " \
                    "does not have. Either the page stopped teaching a refusal status -- read it " \
                    "-- or this scan has stopped recognising the shape; do not leave the clause " \
                    "standing over nothing")
  end

  site = site_checkout
  if site.nil?
    return check("STATUS_PROSE", true,
                 "the page states #{pairs} status-beside-a-code sentence(s); the disclaimer says " \
                 "kiosk.tech/#{SITE_PROBLEM_GUARD} holds them",
                 "NOT CONFIRMED ON THIS RUN -- there is no ../kiosk.tech beside this checkout, so " \
                 "the named guard could not be looked for. This is a pointer into another " \
                 "repository and this leg can only be run where that repository is on disk; the " \
                 "run in the umbrella workspace is the one that answers it")
  end

  guard = File.join(site, SITE_PROBLEM_GUARD)
  ok    = File.file?(guard) && File.executable?(guard)
  check("STATUS_PROSE", ok,
        "the page states #{pairs} status-beside-a-code sentence(s), and kiosk.tech/" \
        "#{SITE_PROBLEM_GUARD} -- the guard this script's disclaimer list names as holding them " \
        "-- is present and executable in the sibling checkout",
        ok ? "vocabulary read from #{PROBLEM_SCHEMA_MIRROR} (#{codes.size} code(s)); guard found " \
             "at #{guard}"
           : "#{guard} is #{File.exist?(guard) ? 'not executable' : 'ABSENT'}. The disclaimer " \
             "list above tells a reader that this page's status prose IS held, one repository " \
             "over, by that script. If it has been renamed or retired, the clause is now " \
             "advertising coverage that does not exist -- which is the defect K-1519 filed, " \
             "pointing the other way. Re-point the clause, or restore the guard")
end

# R1 -- the page's handler class names are the generator's worked example.
def rule_handler_names(page, sources)
  derived = handler_names(sources[:generator]).first
  return vacuous!("HANDLER_NAMES", "no `c.handlers = %w[...]` in #{GENERATOR_INITIALIZER}") if derived.nil? || derived.empty?

  found = handler_names(page_ruby(page))
  return vacuous!("HANDLER_NAMES", "the page declares no `c.handlers = %w[...]` line") if found.empty?

  bad = found.reject { |list| list == derived }
  check("HANDLER_NAMES",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{found.size} c.handlers line(s) name #{derived.join(" ")}",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GENERATOR_INITIALIZER}" : "page names #{bad.map { |b| b.join(" ") }.uniq.join(" | ")}")
end

# The input_schema of one verb, in one source: [required list, property names].
def verb_input_schema(src, verb)
  mask  = masked(src)
  decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
  return nil if decl.nil?

  range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
  return nil if range.nil?

  # `required:` at the TOP LEVEL of the input_schema, located on the masked copy
  # and read off the ORIGINAL (its names live inside string literals); a nested
  # `required:` -- an item schema's, say -- is a different fact and is not this
  # one. `properties:` off the MASKED copy (its names are identifiers, and a
  # brace inside a description sentence must not be counted as structure).
  [top_level_required(mask[range], src[range]), property_names(mask[range])]
end

# R2 -- the worked verb's REQUIRED list is getgrocery's.
def rule_slots_required(page, sources)
  demo = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema in #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT}") if demo.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "no input_schema `required:` on getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB}") if demo[0].nil? || demo[0].empty?

  mine = verb_input_schema(page_ruby(page), WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "the page declares no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema") if mine.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_REQUIRED", "the page's #{WORKED_VERB} declares no input_schema `required:`") if mine[0].nil?

  check("SLOTS_REQUIRED",
        mine[0].sort == demo[0].sort,
        "#{WORKED_VERB} required: #{demo[0].sort.inspect}",
        "page says #{mine[0].sort.inspect} (derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT})")
end

# R3 -- and the same verb declares the same PARAMETERS.
def rule_slots_properties(page, sources)
  demo = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema in #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT}") if demo.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "no input_schema `properties:` on getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB}") if demo[1].nil? || demo[1].empty?

  mine = verb_input_schema(page_ruby(page), WORKED_VERB)
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "the page declares no `#{WORKED_VERB}` input_schema") if mine.nil?
  return vacuous!("SLOTS_PROPERTIES", "the page's #{WORKED_VERB} declares no input_schema `properties:`") if mine[1].nil?

  check("SLOTS_PROPERTIES",
        mine[1].sort == demo[1].sort,
        "#{WORKED_VERB} parameters: #{demo[1].sort.inspect}",
        "page declares #{mine[1].sort.inspect} (derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT})")
end

# R4 -- BAN. No declared-integer argument is read with `.to_i`.
#
# Over the page's Ruby with COMMENTS MASKED, deliberately: the page explains
# why `.to_i` is not the spelling it uses, and quoting the idiom in order to
# refuse it is the opposite of teaching it. What is banned is the executable
# line, which is the only thing an operator copies.
def rule_integer_coercion(page, sources)
  names = integer_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | integer_param_names(sources[:orders])
  return vacuous!("INTEGER_COERCION", "getgrocery declares no `type: \"integer\"` input parameter") if names.empty?

  code = masked(page_ruby(page))
  live = names.select { |n| code.include?(n) }
  if live.empty?
    return vacuous!("INTEGER_COERCION",
                    "none of getgrocery's integer parameters (#{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")}) is read in the page's Ruby")
  end

  pattern = /\[:(?:#{live.map { |n| Regexp.escape(n) }.join("|")})\]\s*(?:&\.|\.)to_i\b/
  hits    = code.scan(pattern)
  check("INTEGER_COERCION",
        hits.empty?,
        "no `.to_i` on #{live.sort.join(", ")} (#{live.size} live of #{names.size} derived)",
        hits.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : "#{hits.size} site(s): #{hits.uniq.join(", ")}")
end

# R5 -- BAN. No frozen calendar literal, anywhere on the page.
def rule_frozen_date(page, _sources)
  unless page.include?("example_params(")
    return vacuous!("FROZEN_DATE", "the page declares no `example_params(`, so there is nothing this ban protects")
  end

  hits = page.scan(/(?<![0-9a-fA-F-])20[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}/)
  check("FROZEN_DATE",
        hits.empty?,
        "no YYYY-MM-DD literal on the page",
        hits.empty? ? "a literal ages into the 400 the verb answers a past date with" : "#{hits.size} literal(s): #{hits.uniq.join(", ")}")
end

# R6 -- the page must NAME getgrocery's real handler classes wherever they
# differ from the ones it teaches, so it cannot present one as the other.
def rule_getgrocery_handlers(page, sources)
  demo = handler_names(sources[:getgrocery_init]).first
  return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "no `c.handlers = %w[...]` in #{GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER}") if demo.nil? || demo.empty?

  mine = handler_names(page_ruby(page)).first
  return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "the page declares no `c.handlers = %w[...]` line") if mine.nil? || mine.empty?

  if demo.sort == mine.sort
    return vacuous!("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS",
                    "getgrocery now registers exactly the classes the page teaches (#{demo.join(" ")}); " \
                    "this rule held the DIFFERENCE and now holds nothing -- restate it or delete it")
  end

  divergent = demo - mine
  missing   = divergent.reject { |c| page.include?(c) }
  check("GETGROCERY_HANDLERS",
        missing.empty?,
        "the page names getgrocery's own #{divergent.join(", ")}",
        missing.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER}" : "not named on the page: #{missing.join(", ")}")
end

# The (required, properties) pair getgrocery declares for a verb, wherever the
# verb lives -- the read surface or the write surface. One lookup, so a rule
# reaches every verb the two controllers declare instead of one named constant.
def demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
  verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], verb) || verb_input_schema(sources[:orders], verb)
end

# Every verb BOTH getgrocery and the page declare. The subject list for the
# verb-by-verb rules below, derived on both sides: a verb renamed in either
# place leaves the set rather than silently keeping a stale comparison alive.
def shared_verbs(page, sources)
  demo = declared_verbs(sources[:storefront]) | declared_verbs(sources[:orders])
  demo & declared_verbs(page_ruby(page))
end

# R7 -- BAN. The page must not make required, in the HANDLER, an argument
# getgrocery's own declaration leaves optional. This is the half of K-1325 that
# lived in code rather than in the schema.
#
# EVERY SHARED VERB, EACH SCOPED TO ITS OWN HANDLER BODY (K-1338). It used to
# derive from one named verb and scan the whole page, which could not have
# reached the write verbs -- and must not be widened by unioning the optional
# sets either: `delivery_address` is optional on `reschedule_delivery` and
# REQUIRED on `create_order`, so a page-wide ban on requiring it would be red
# for doing the right thing one verb over.
def rule_no_require_optional(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  mask     = masked(page_ruby(page))
  subjects = 0
  hits     = []
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
    next if demo.nil?

    req, props = demo
    next if req.nil? || props.nil?

    optional = props - req
    next if optional.empty?

    body = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if body.nil?

    subjects += optional.size
    pattern = /params\.require\(:(?:#{optional.map { |n| Regexp.escape(n) }.join("|")})\)/
    mask[body].scan(pattern) { hits << "#{verb}: #{Regexp.last_match(0)}" }
  end

  if subjects.zero?
    return vacuous!("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",
                    "no verb of #{shared.join(", ")} leaves a declared parameter optional in getgrocery, " \
                    "so this ban has no subject")
  end

  check("REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",
        hits.empty?,
        "no `params.require` on the #{subjects} optional argument(s) of #{shared.sort.join(", ")}",
        hits.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : hits.join(" | "))
end

# R11 -- every verb both sides declare REQUIRES what getgrocery requires, and
# nothing more (K-1338).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page required `delivery_date` on BOTH write verbs
# where getgrocery requires it on neither -- omitting it books tomorrow, for the
# same reason `delivery_slots` leaves its own `date` optional (the caller cannot
# compute the operator's today). An operator copying the page shipped a stricter
# contract than the origin it was copied from, and the schema half of K-1325 one
# verb over had no rule reaching it.
#
# TWO DIRECTIONS, because one of them alone is half a comparison:
#   OVER   the page requires a name getgrocery does not -- the K-1338 shape.
#   UNDER  the page DECLARES a parameter getgrocery requires and leaves it
#          optional -- a cart an operator's own origin would refuse.
# A parameter the page does not declare at all is neither: the page teaches a
# deliberately smaller surface than the origin it is derived from, and that is
# a simplification rather than the two sides disagreeing.
def rule_write_required(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("WRITE_REQUIRED", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  ruby     = page_ruby(page)
  compared = []
  derived  = 0
  bad      = []
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_input_schema(sources, verb)
    mine = verb_input_schema(ruby, verb)
    next if demo.nil? || mine.nil?

    dreq, = demo
    mreq, mprops = mine
    next if dreq.nil? || mreq.nil? || mprops.nil?

    compared << verb
    derived  += dreq.size
    over  = mreq - dreq
    under = (dreq & mprops) - mreq
    bad << "#{verb} requires #{over.sort.inspect}, which getgrocery leaves optional" if over.any?
    bad << "#{verb} leaves #{under.sort.inspect} optional, which getgrocery requires" if under.any?
  end

  if compared.empty? || derived.zero?
    return vacuous!("WRITE_REQUIRED",
                    "none of #{shared.join(", ")} declares a non-empty input_schema `required:` on " \
                    "getgrocery's side, so there is nothing to hold the page to")
  end

  check("WRITE_REQUIRED",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{compared.size} shared verb(s) (#{compared.sort.join(", ")}) require exactly what getgrocery requires " \
        "(#{derived} name(s) derived)",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : bad.join(" | "))
end

# R12 -- BAN. A handler that indexes the elements of a declared ARRAY OF
# OBJECTS must check the element IS one first (K-1339).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page's `create_order` ran `item[:sku]` over every
# cart entry with no shape check, so `items: ["bread"]` -- a well-formed request
# with the wrong shape, which an assistant does send -- raised inside the
# handler and reached the caller as a 500 about the operator's server.
# getgrocery answers a typed 400 naming the shape it wanted. The page is what an
# operator copies, so the page taught the 500.
#
# The subject list is DERIVED: every input parameter getgrocery declares whose
# own body says both `type: "array"` and `type: "object"`. A site is a page verb
# whose handler body reads `params[:<that name>]`; the guard the site must carry
# is `is_a?(Hash)`, read off the MASKED body so a comment explaining the idiom
# cannot stand in for running it.
def rule_item_shape(page, sources)
  names = array_of_object_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | array_of_object_param_names(sources[:orders])
  return vacuous!("ITEM_SHAPE", "getgrocery declares no array-of-objects input parameter") if names.empty?

  ruby  = page_ruby(page)
  mask  = masked(ruby)
  sites = []
  declared_verbs(ruby).each do |verb|
    body = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if body.nil?

    text = mask[body]
    names.each do |name|
      next unless text.include?("params[:#{name}]")

      sites << [verb, name, text.include?("is_a?(Hash)")]
    end
  end

  if sites.empty?
    return vacuous!("ITEM_SHAPE",
                    "no page handler reads any of getgrocery's array-of-objects parameters " \
                    "(#{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")})")
  end

  missing = sites.reject { |(_v, _n, ok)| ok }
  check("ITEM_SHAPE",
        missing.empty?,
        "#{sites.size} site(s) reading #{names.to_a.sort.join(", ")} guard the element with `is_a?(Hash)`",
        missing.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : "unguarded: #{missing.map { |(v, n, _)| "#{v}/#{n}" }.join(", ")}")
end

# R8 -- the page states the Rails floor the engine's gemspec declares.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (K-1320): `kiosk-server` requires Rails `~> 8.1`, which
# excludes 7.x and 8.0.x -- most of the "existing Rails apps" this page is
# addressed to -- and the page, whose whole framing is "assuming a working Rails
# + Postgres app you already know", never mentioned the one constraint most
# likely to disqualify the reader's app. Bundler's refusal is the only place it
# was stated, at the end of `bundle install`.
def rule_rails_floor(page, sources)
  floor = rails_floor(sources[:server_gemspec])
  if floor.nil?
    return vacuous!("RAILS_FLOOR",
                    "#{SERVER_GEMSPEC} declares no single `~> MAJOR.MINOR` shared by " \
                    "#{RAILS_COMPONENTS.join(", ")}")
  end
  return vacuous!("RAILS_FLOOR", "the page does not mention Rails at all") unless page.include?("Rails")

  hits = page.scan("Rails #{floor}").length
  check("RAILS_FLOOR",
        hits.positive?,
        "the page names Rails #{floor} (#{hits} occurrence(s))",
        hits.positive? ? "derived from #{SERVER_GEMSPEC}" : "the page names Rails but never `Rails #{floor}`")
end

# R9 -- and the Ruby floor every gemspec declares.
def rule_ruby_floor(page, sources)
  floor = ruby_floor(sources[:core_gemspec])
  return vacuous!("RUBY_FLOOR", "#{CORE_GEMSPEC} declares no `required_ruby_version = \">= X.Y.Z\"`") if floor.nil?
  return vacuous!("RUBY_FLOOR", "the page does not mention Ruby at all") unless page.include?("Ruby")

  hits = page.scan("Ruby #{floor}").length
  check("RUBY_FLOOR",
        hits.positive?,
        "the page names Ruby #{floor} (#{hits} occurrence(s))",
        hits.positive? ? "derived from #{CORE_GEMSPEC}" : "the page names Ruby but never `Ruby #{floor}`")
end

# R10 -- every Kiosk gem the page tells an operator to add carries the SOURCE
# QUALIFIER the monorepo README declares canonical.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (K-1319): four different install instructions lived
# across the two published repositories, two of them resolving against RubyGems,
# where no Kiosk gem is published -- and nothing said so in either direction.
# Publication status and the canonical form are now stated ONCE, in the
# monorepo README; this holds the page to that one statement instead of to a
# second copy of it.
def rule_install_source(page, sources)
  canonical = canonical_install_source(sources[:readme])
  if canonical.nil?
    return vacuous!("INSTALL_SOURCE",
                    "#{MONOREPO_README} declares no canonical `gem \"kiosk-all\", <source>: \"...\"` " \
                    "line -- if the gems have been published and the qualifier is gone, restate " \
                    "this rule rather than leaving it matching nothing")
  end

  key, value = canonical
  lines = page_gem_lines(page)
  return vacuous!("INSTALL_SOURCE", "the page's Ruby carries no `gem \"kiosk-...\"` line") if lines.empty?

  qualifier = "#{key}: \"#{value}\""
  bad = lines.reject { |l| l.include?(qualifier) }
  check("INSTALL_SOURCE",
        bad.empty?,
        "all #{lines.length} `gem \"kiosk-*\"` line(s) carry `#{qualifier}`",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{MONOREPO_README}" : "#{bad.length} without it: #{bad.map(&:strip).join(" | ")}")
end

# ── K-1341 / K-1344 derivations ─────────────────────────────────────────────

# The rescue seam's body: `def kiosk_rescue_to_wire` down to its own `end`, by
# indentation. Read off the MASKED copy for structure and returned from the
# ORIGINAL, the same discipline every reader above uses.
def rescue_seam_body(src)
  mask = masked(src)
  # `[ \t]*` and NOT `\s*`: `masked` blanks a comment to SPACES, so the
  # forty-line header above this def is whitespace in the mask and a `\s*`
  # that crosses newlines matches at the top of it -- which makes `indent` a
  # multi-line blob and the closing `end` unfindable. Measured: the first
  # writing of this reader answered VACUOUS on a seam that is plainly there.
  at   = mask.index(/^[ \t]*def kiosk_rescue_to_wire\b/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  indent = mask[at..].match(/\A([ \t]*)def/)[1]
  stop   = mask.index(/^#{indent}end$/, at + 1)
  return nil if stop.nil?

  src[at..stop]
end

# The class the seam re-raises UNTOUCHED — `raise exception if
# exception.is_a?(X)`. That class is the operator's deliberate route to the
# assistant, so the page must name it; deriving it means a rename in the engine
# reddens the page rather than leaving a stale class name published.
def reraised_class(seam)
  return nil if seam.nil?

  m = seam.match(/raise\s+\w+\s+if\s+\w+\.is_a\?\(([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_:]*)\)/)
  m && m[1]
end

# Every sentence `Errors.rescued_wire` gives the CALLER in place of the
# exception's own, as {code => hint}. Ruby's adjacent-string continuation is
# joined, because that is how the constant is written and a half-sentence would
# never match a page.
def rescued_hints(src)
  body = src[/RESCUED_HINTS\s*=\s*\{(.*?)\}\.freeze/m, 1]
  return {} if body.nil?

  out = {}
  body.scan(/"([a-z_]+)"\s*=>\s*((?:"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"\s*\\?\s*)+),/m) do |code, literal|
    out[code] = literal.scan(/"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"/).flatten.join
  end
  out
end

# R13 -- the page teaches «raise what you would raise anyway»; the engine no
# longer publishes what that raise SAID, and the page must carry both halves
# of that (K-1341, closing K-1310's blast radius one repository over).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: after K-1310 the rescue seam renders
# {Errors.rescued_wire}'s sentence and hint for the code it decided, and sends
# the exception's class, message and backtrace to the operator's log instead of
# the wire. The page's paragraph stayed ACCURATE — it only ever claimed the
# STATUS maps to a code — and stopped being COMPLETE: an operator reading it
# learned the raise-and-map recovery and not that the sentence they raised with
# is dropped, nor that rendering the envelope or raising a Kiosk error class is
# how they speak to the assistant on purpose. `kiosk-server/README.md` gained
# exactly that split in the same wave and the published page did not.
#
# TWO DERIVED HALVES, because either alone is half the instruction:
#   WHAT ARRIVES INSTEAD  one of {Errors::RESCUED_HINTS}' sentences, verbatim.
#   THE DELIBERATE ROUTE  the class the seam re-raises untouched, by name.
#
# AND A THIRD STATE THAT IS NEITHER: if the seam ever renders the exception's
# own message again, this rule's subject is gone and the page's paragraph
# becomes FALSE rather than merely incomplete. That is reported as VACUOUS —
# red, naming itself — so the page is re-decided rather than quietly held to a
# fact that stopped being one.
def rule_error_redaction(page, sources)
  seam = rescue_seam_body(sources[:handler_mixin])
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "no `def kiosk_rescue_to_wire` in #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}") if seam.nil?

  if seam.match?(/\w+\.message/)
    return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION",
                    "#{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s rescue seam renders an exception's own `.message` again, so " \
                    "there is no redaction for the page to teach -- the page's paragraph is now FALSE and " \
                    "this rule must be re-decided, not deleted")
  end

  klass = reraised_class(seam)
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "#{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s seam re-raises no class untouched") if klass.nil?

  hints = rescued_hints(sources[:server_errors])
  return vacuous!("ERROR_REDACTION", "no RESCUED_HINTS map in #{SERVER_ERRORS}") if hints.empty?

  # The page may quote any ONE of them -- it teaches one worked refusal, not
  # the whole table -- but it must quote one exactly, or it is paraphrasing a
  # sentence this engine sends.
  quoted = hints.select { |_code, hint| page.include?(hint) }
  # `Errors::Base` is the ancestor the seam tests; an operator raises a
  # SUBCLASS, so the page is held to the namespace and either spelling passes.
  needle = klass.sub(/::Base\z/, "")
  named  = page.include?(needle)

  bad = []
  bad << "the page quotes none of the #{hints.size} `RESCUED_HINTS` sentences the wire sends in place of " \
         "the exception's own" if quoted.empty?
  bad << "the page never names #{needle}, the class #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN}'s seam re-raises untouched -- " \
         "so it does not say how an operator speaks to the assistant deliberately" unless named

  check("ERROR_REDACTION",
        bad.empty?,
        "the page carries the redaction split: #{quoted.keys.sort.join(", ")} hint(s) quoted verbatim, " \
        "#{needle} named",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN} + #{SERVER_ERRORS}" : bad.join(" | "))
end

# The JSON Schema assertion keywords a property declaration can carry. This
# list is the STANDARD's vocabulary, not a fact about this repository -- what
# is derived is which of them getgrocery actually uses, on which property, with
# what value, and a keyword nobody declares contributes nothing.
ASSERTION_KEYWORDS = %w[format pattern enum const minimum maximum exclusiveMinimum
                        exclusiveMaximum multipleOf minLength maxLength minItems
                        maxItems uniqueItems].freeze

# A property's OWN assertion keywords, as {keyword => value-as-written}.
# DEPTH 1 ONLY: `items:`'s body contains the element schema, whose `qty` has a
# `maximum` of its own, and reading that as the array's would compare two facts
# that are not the same fact -- the identical shape `top_level_required` exists
# for one macro up.
KEYWORD_AT = /\G([a-zA-Z]+):[ \t]*/

def property_assertions(src, base)
  mask  = masked(src)
  out   = {}
  property_body_ranges(mask, base).each do |name, body|
    pairs = {}
    depth = 0
    i     = body.first
    while i <= body.last
      c = mask[i]
      if ["{", "["].include?(c)
        depth += 1
      elsif ["}", "]"].include?(c)
        depth -= 1
        break if depth.zero? && i > body.first
      elsif depth == 1 && (i.zero? || mask[i - 1] =~ /[\s{,]/) &&
            # `\G` anchors the match AT `i` without slicing the string: the
            # first writing sliced `mask[i..body.last]` on every character,
            # which is quadratic and made the self-test take minutes.
            (m = KEYWORD_AT.match(mask, i)) && ASSERTION_KEYWORDS.include?(m[1])
        pairs[m[1]] = assertion_value(src[(i + m[0].length)..body.last])
      end
      i += 1
    end
    out[name] = pairs
  end
  out
end

# The value as WRITTEN, so `maximum: 6` and `maximum: WireArguments::MAX_INT4`
# stay distinguishable. A bracketed value is read to its matching `]` (an
# `enum: ["a", "b"]` has a comma inside it); anything else runs to the comma or
# the end of the line.
def assertion_value(text)
  return "" if text.nil?

  if text.start_with?("[")
    depth = 0
    i     = 0
    while i < text.length
      depth += 1 if text[i] == "["
      if text[i] == "]"
        depth -= 1
        break if depth.zero?
      end
      i += 1
    end
    text[0..i]
  else
    text[/\A[^,\n]*/].to_s.strip
  end
end

# A value written out in the schema's own vocabulary -- a string, a number, an
# array, a boolean -- as opposed to a Ruby name only the demo's own class tree
# can resolve.
def literal_assertion?(value)
  value.match?(/\A("|\d|-|\[|true\b|false\b)/)
end

# A Ruby constant reference as getgrocery writes one inside a declaration:
# `Kiosk::UuidCheck::JSON_SCHEMA_PATTERN`, `WireArguments::MAX_INT4`. Scoped form
# only, which is the only form these declarations use -- a bare `MAX_INT4` would
# be a constant of the controller itself and is deliberately not recognised, so
# it reads as UNRESOLVED rather than being guessed at. The scope may be NESTED,
# because a constant that ships in a gem is namespaced and the declaration cites
# it the way Ruby resolves it.
CONSTANT_REF = /\A[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:::[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*::[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*\z/

def constant_reference?(value)
  value.match?(CONSTANT_REF)
end

# Every `CONST = <literal>` in one file, keyed `Class::CONST`. Structure is read
# off the masked copy and the value off the original, the same discipline every
# other reader here uses: `masked` blanks a comment to spaces without moving a
# byte, so rstripping the masked line is where the CODE ends, and a `#` inside
# the string being assigned cannot truncate it.
def constant_literals(src)
  # The nesting is tracked by INDENTATION rather than by matching `end`: these
  # are conventionally-indented files, and a declaration cites a constant the
  # way Ruby resolves it, so `module Kiosk` / `module UuidCheck` has to key
  # `Kiosk::UuidCheck::CONST` and not only `UuidCheck::CONST`. Both spellings
  # are emitted, so a declaration may name the full path or the innermost scope
  # alone and this table answers either.
  stack = []
  out   = {}
  masked(src).lines.zip(src.lines).each do |mline, oline|
    if (m = mline.match(/\A(\s*)(?:class|module)\s+([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_:]*)/))
      indent = m[1].length
      stack.pop while stack.any? && stack.last[0] >= indent
      stack << [indent, m[2]]
    elsif stack.any? && (m = mline.match(/\A\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*)\s*=\s*/))
      value = oline[m[0].length...mline.rstrip.length].to_s.strip.sub(/\.freeze\z/, "").strip
      next if value.empty?

      names = stack.map(&:last)
      out["#{names.join('::')}::#{m[1]}"] = value
      out["#{names.last}::#{m[1]}"] = value
    end
  end
  out
end

# The constant table R14 resolves getgrocery's declarations against. Two files,
# because that is where the two constants live; a third would be added here and
# nowhere else.
def demo_constants(sources)
  constant_literals(sources[:uuid_check]).merge(constant_literals(sources[:wire_arguments]))
end

# `2_147_483_647` and `2147483647` are the same ceiling written twice. Nothing
# else is normalised: a string's quoting and spelling are part of the value.
def canonical_assertion(value)
  v = value.to_s.strip
  v.match?(/\A-?\d[\d_]*\z/) ? v.delete("_") : v
end

# The ELEMENT schema of an array property -- the `items: {...}` inside a
# property's own body -- as {name => keywords}. `qty`'s ceiling lives here and
# nowhere else, so a depth-1 reader cannot see it at all: it was invisible to
# this rule, not merely uncompared, for as long as the rule stopped at depth 1.
# The DEPTH-1 note on `property_assertions` still stands and this does not
# violate it -- that note forbids reading the element's `maximum` AS THE ARRAY'S,
# which conflates two facts. Keying it `items[].qty` keeps them apart, and
# compares each side's element schema with the other side's.
def element_property_assertions(src, prop_range)
  slice = masked(src)[prop_range]
  at    = slice.index(/items:\s*\{/)
  return {} if at.nil?

  open  = slice.index("{", at)
  depth = 0
  j     = open
  while j < slice.length
    depth += 1 if slice[j] == "{"
    if slice[j] == "}"
      depth -= 1
      break if depth.zero?
    end
    j += 1
  end
  return {} unless depth.zero?

  property_assertions(src, (prop_range.first + open)..(prop_range.first + j))
end

# Every property of one `input_schema`, its array elements included.
def property_assertions_deep(src, range)
  out  = property_assertions(src, range)
  mask = masked(src)
  property_body_ranges(mask, range).each do |name, body|
    element_property_assertions(src, body).each { |sub, kws| out["#{name}[].#{sub}"] = kws }
  end
  out
end

def demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
  [sources[:storefront], sources[:orders]].each do |src|
    mask  = masked(src)
    decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
    next if decl.nil?

    range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
    next if range.nil?

    return property_assertions_deep(src, range)
  end
  nil
end

def page_property_assertions(ruby, verb)
  mask  = masked(ruby)
  decl  = declaration_range(mask, verb)
  return nil if decl.nil?

  range = input_schema_range(mask, decl)
  return nil if range.nil?

  property_assertions_deep(ruby, range)
end

# R14 -- every property both sides declare carries the same VALIDATION
# KEYWORDS, so the page cannot publish a shape the origin does not perform
# (K-1344).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page declared `format: "date"` on `delivery_date`
# for both write verbs where getgrocery declares no `format` there at all --
# deliberately, because that argument is parsed with `Date.parse` and a bare
# "20260101" is published behaviour on that verb pair (K-1324). MEASURED with
# json_schemer 2.5.0, which asserts `format` by default: the page's schema
# refuses "20260101" with a wire 400 before the handler runs, and the handler
# the page shows beside it is written to accept it. So an operator copying the
# page shipped a schema stricter than its own handler and than the reference.
# Eleven rules preceded this one and not one compared a property's KEYWORDS.
#
# TWO DIRECTIONS, and they are not symmetrical:
#   OVER   the page declares a keyword (or a different value) getgrocery does
#          not -- the K-1344 shape, and the one that REFUSES real input.
#   UNDER  the page omits a keyword getgrocery declares -- a copied schema
#          LOOSER than the origin, which is the direction K-1354 found: the page
#          accepts what the origin refuses, so an operator copying it ships a
#          verb whose own handler was never written to take that input. The page
#          had no ceiling on `create_order`'s `qty` and no `pattern` on
#          `reschedule_delivery`'s `order_id`, and its handler answers the first
#          of those with the 500 K-1047 recorded on the origin.
#
# UNDER USED TO BE SCOPED TO LITERALS, AND THAT SCOPING WAS THE HOLE (K-1354).
# getgrocery writes `pattern: Kiosk::UuidCheck::JSON_SCHEMA_PATTERN` and
# `maximum: WireArguments::MAX_INT4` -- constants belonging to classes the page's
# standalone controller does not have -- so the first writing COUNTED AND PRINTED
# those instead of asserting on them. A rule that prints is a rule nothing holds:
# the printed line said `reschedule_delivery.order_id.pattern` was not compared,
# and it was read as a note rather than as the gap it was, for as long as it stood.
# So the constant is RESOLVED to the literal it is assigned, from the file that
# assigns it, and the page is held to that literal -- which it now inlines,
# because a schema is the one place a shape has to be written in the schema's own
# vocabulary. A constant this rule CANNOT resolve is an ERROR naming it, never a
# note: the one thing that may not happen here is a silent pass.
#
# AND THE OTHER HALF OF K-1354 WAS NOT UNCOMPARED BUT INVISIBLE. `qty`'s ceiling
# is declared inside `items:`'s ELEMENT schema, and this rule read depth 1 only,
# so it never saw the property at all -- it was not in the printed list either.
# `property_assertions_deep` keys the element's properties `items[].qty`, which
# compares each side's element schema against the other's without ever reading an
# element's `maximum` as the array's.
def rule_property_keywords(page, sources)
  shared = shared_verbs(page, sources)
  return vacuous!("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS", "no verb is declared on both sides") if shared.empty?

  consts    = demo_constants(sources)
  ruby      = page_ruby(page)
  pairs     = []            # [verb, prop, demo_assertions, page_assertions]
  derived   = 0
  shared.each do |verb|
    demo = demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
    mine = page_property_assertions(ruby, verb)
    next if demo.nil? || mine.nil?

    (demo.keys & mine.keys).sort.each do |prop|
      derived += demo[prop].size
      pairs << [verb, prop, demo[prop], mine[prop]]
    end
  end

  if pairs.empty? || derived.zero?
    return vacuous!("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",
                    "no property shared by #{shared.sort.join(", ")} carries a JSON Schema assertion " \
                    "keyword on getgrocery's side, so there is nothing to hold the page to")
  end

  bad      = []
  resolved = []
  pairs.each do |verb, prop, demo, mine|
    # getgrocery's side first, one property at a time: every constant it writes
    # becomes the literal it is assigned, or the rule says so and fails.
    want = {}
    demo.each do |kw, value|
      if constant_reference?(value)
        literal = consts[value]
        if literal.nil?
          bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} declares #{kw}: #{value} in #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} / " \
                 "#{GETGROCERY_ORDERS} and this rule could not resolve that constant to a literal " \
                 "in #{CORE_UUID_CHECK} or #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS}. It is not compared, " \
                 "which is the state K-1354 closed -- re-point the resolution, do not drop the keyword"
          next
        end
        resolved << "#{verb}.#{prop}.#{kw} = #{value}"
        want[kw] = literal
      else
        want[kw] = value
      end
    end

    (mine.keys - want.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} declares #{kw}: #{mine[kw]}, which getgrocery does not declare at all"
    end
    (mine.keys & want.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      next if canonical_assertion(mine[kw]) == canonical_assertion(want[kw])

      bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} declares #{kw}: #{mine[kw]} where getgrocery declares #{want[kw]}"
    end
    (want.keys - mine.keys).sort.each do |kw|
      bad << "#{verb}.#{prop} omits #{kw}: #{want[kw]}, which getgrocery declares"
    end
  end

  detail = bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS}" : bad.join(" | ")
  unless resolved.empty?
    detail += "; #{resolved.size} keyword(s) compared through a resolved getgrocery constant: " \
              "#{resolved.join(", ")}"
  end
  check("PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{pairs.size} shared propert(ies) across #{shared.size} verb(s) carry getgrocery's keywords " \
        "(#{derived} keyword(s) derived)",
        detail)
end

# ── R15's derivation chain ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The origin's total guard, resolved LINK BY LINK out of this repository rather
# than named here: the local `create_order` binds its summed cart total to, the
# module function that local is handed to, the constant that function bounds it
# against, and the literal that constant is assigned. Nothing in this chain is
# typed, so renaming any link reddens the rule instead of quietly emptying it --
# and a link that does not resolve is reported as the reason the rule holds
# nothing, never passed over. Same discipline R14 adopted for its constants.
#
# @return [Array(String, String, nil), Array(nil, nil, String)] ceiling +
#   provenance, or the reason there is none.
def derived_total_ceiling(sources)
  op   = sources[:create_order_op]
  mask = masked(op)
  bind = mask.match(/^[ \t]*([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)[ \t]*=[^\n]*\.sum\b/)
  return [nil, nil, "#{GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER} binds no summed total to a local"] if bind.nil?

  local = bind[1]
  call  = mask.match(/([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\.([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)\(\s*#{Regexp.escape(local)}\s*\)/)
  return [nil, nil, "nothing in #{GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER} hands `#{local}` to a guard"] if call.nil?

  recv, meth = call[1], call[2]
  wmask = masked(sources[:wire_arguments])
  at    = wmask.index(/^[ \t]*def #{Regexp.escape(meth)}\b/)
  return [nil, nil, "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS} declares no `def #{meth}`"] if at.nil?

  stop = wmask.index(/^[ \t]*def /, at + 1) || wmask.length
  cmp  = wmask[at...stop].match(/(?:<=|<|>=|>)\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]+)\b/)
  return [nil, nil, "`#{recv}.#{meth}` bounds `#{local}` by no constant"] if cmp.nil?

  key     = "#{recv}::#{cmp[1]}"
  literal = demo_constants(sources)[key]
  return [nil, nil, "`#{key}` resolves to no literal in #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS}"] if literal.nil?

  [canonical_assertion(literal),
   "#{GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER} `#{local} = ....sum` -> #{recv}.#{meth} -> #{key} = #{literal}",
   nil]
end

# `NAME = <literal>` in the page's OWN Ruby, keyed by the bare name. The page's
# controller is standalone by design and can name no constant of the demo's, so
# a ceiling it guards with is either an inline literal or a constant it assigns
# itself; both resolve here, and a name that resolves to NEITHER is an error
# rather than a pass -- R14's rule about unresolvable constants, on the other
# side of the boundary.
def page_constants(ruby)
  out = {}
  masked(ruby).lines.zip(ruby.lines).each do |mline, oline|
    next unless (m = mline.match(/\A\s*([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]+)\s*=\s*/))

    value = oline[m[0].length...mline.rstrip.length].to_s.strip
    out[m[1]] = value unless value.empty?
  end
  out
end

# Every page handler that SUMS a total, with the coordinates R15 judges and its
# self-test plants aim at: what the sum is bound to, what that local is compared
# against, and whether that comparison is the FIRST thing to touch the local.
# One function so a plant can never aim at something the rule does not read.
def page_total_sites(ruby)
  mask = masked(ruby)
  declared_verbs(ruby).filter_map do |verb|
    range = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if range.nil?

    body = mask[range]
    next unless body.match?(/\.sum\b/)

    bind = body.match(/^[ \t]*([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)[ \t]*=[^\n]*\.sum\b/)
    next({ verb: verb, local: nil, token: nil, guard_first: false }) if bind.nil?

    local = bind[1]
    tail  = body[bind.end(0)..].to_s
    cmp   = tail.match(/\b#{Regexp.escape(local)}\b\s*(?:>=|>|<=|<)\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*|\d[\d_]*)/)
    { verb: verb, local: local, token: cmp && cmp[1],
      guard_first: !cmp.nil? && tail.index(/\b#{Regexp.escape(local)}\b/) == cmp.begin(0) }
  end
end

# R15 -- the page REFUSES a cart it cannot price, at the origin's own ceiling
# and before anything is written (K-1359, which is K-1047's other half).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES, and why no rule above could reach it. K-1354 gave the
# page `qty`'s declared `maximum`, and R14 holds it there. That ceiling is per
# LINE and the overflow is in the SUM: at the catalogue's cheapest row it takes
# tens of millions of units to pass `orders.total_cents`, and every one of those
# quantities is inside the `maximum` the page declares. So the page went on
# computing `price_cents * qty` summed straight into `Order.create!`, where
# activemodel raises `ActiveModel::RangeError` in RUBY before any SQL and the
# wire answers 500 -- the identical shape K-1047 recorded on the origin and
# closed there with `WireArguments.priceable_total`.
#
# WHY A NEW RULE RATHER THAN AN ARM OF R14. R14's subject is a DECLARED
# PROPERTY: it walks each side's `properties:` hashes and compares the assertion
# keywords found there. A cart total is not a property of anything -- it is a
# value the handler COMPUTES from the operator's own catalogue prices, and JSON
# Schema has no per-property keyword that could express a bound on it. That is
# not an accident of R14's implementation; it is the same reason getgrocery's
# own guard is a handler refusal and not a `maximum`, stated in `wire_arguments.rb`
# and in `create_order`'s description. Extending R14 would have meant giving one
# rule two unrelated subjects and two unrelated derivations, and the printed line
# «12 shared properties carry getgrocery's keywords» would then have been true of
# a run that also silently asserted something about a handler body. So: a rule of
# its own, in the family of R4 and R12, which already read handler bodies.
#
# WHAT IT ASSERTS, per summing handler, and why each conjunct is load-bearing:
#   BOUND      the sum is bound to a local. A sum written straight into the call
#              that writes it is unrefusable by construction -- the pre-fix shape.
#   CEILING    that local is compared against the ceiling DERIVED above. A
#              different number is an invented basket size; the bound is the
#              column's.
#   ORDER      the comparison is the FIRST use of the local. A guard the value
#              has already been consumed past is not a guard, and this is the
#              half the origin states by taking its refusal the moment prices
#              resolve and BEFORE the replace path locks a row.
def rule_total_guard(page, sources)
  ceiling, provenance, why = derived_total_ceiling(sources)
  return vacuous!("TOTAL_GUARD", why) if ceiling.nil?

  ruby   = page_ruby(page)
  sites  = page_total_sites(ruby)
  if sites.empty?
    return vacuous!("TOTAL_GUARD",
                    "no handler in the page's Ruby sums a total, so there is nothing to hold to " \
                    "the ceiling derived from #{GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER}")
  end

  consts = page_constants(ruby)
  bad    = []
  sites.each do |site|
    verb = site[:verb]
    if site[:local].nil?
      bad << "#{verb} sums its total straight into the value it writes, so nothing can refuse " \
             "the cart first -- bind the sum to a local and guard the local"
      next
    end
    if site[:token].nil?
      bad << "#{verb} binds its summed total to `#{site[:local]}` and never compares it to a ceiling"
      next
    end
    unless site[:guard_first]
      bad << "#{verb} uses `#{site[:local]}` before the ceiling comparison, so the total is " \
             "consumed before it is refused"
      next
    end
    token = site[:token]
    value = token.match?(/\A\d/) ? token : consts[token]
    if value.nil?
      bad << "#{verb} bounds `#{site[:local]}` by `#{token}`, which resolves to no literal in the " \
             "page's own Ruby -- assign it on the page or inline the number, do not drop the bound"
      next
    end
    next if canonical_assertion(value) == ceiling

    bad << "#{verb} bounds `#{site[:local]}` at #{value} where getgrocery bounds the same sum at #{ceiling}"
  end

  check("TOTAL_GUARD",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{sites.size} summing handler(s) (#{sites.map { |s| s[:verb] }.sort.join(", ")}) refuse an " \
        "unpriceable cart at #{ceiling} before anything is written",
        bad.empty? ? provenance : bad.join(" | "))
end

# ── R16's derivation chain ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# «A delivery day that is already gone is refused, and the window about to be
# booked is re-checked against the clock» — walked out of this repository LINK
# BY LINK, so no sentence, verb name, module name or method name below is typed
# here (K-1366):
#
#   controller   def <verb> … <Class>.call(…)      -> the Operation it delegates to
#   operations/  WireArguments.<fn>(…)             -> the guards that verb takes
#   guard module def <fn> … DeliverySlots.<m>      -> the clock those guards read
#   slot module  def past?(…, at: now)             -> the CLOCK reader (a keyword
#                                                     default naming a nullary
#                                                     method of the same module)
#                slot_at(date, slot) <= at         -> the WINDOW reader
#   guard module DeliverySlots.now.to_date         -> the DAY expression
#
# THE PAGE'S CONTROLLER IS STANDALONE by design and can name no `WireArguments`
# of the demo's, so what it is held to is the COMPARISON, not the call: the day
# expression must be an operand of one, and the window expression must be
# compared with the clock. Both spellings a handler actually uses count — the
# expression written at the comparison, or a local the same body binds to it —
# because the origin's own operation binds `slot_at` to a local and hands it on,
# and a rule that accepted only one of those would be a rule about formatting.

# The first `module`/`class` a source declares — the name its callers spell.
def declared_module(src)
  m = masked(src).match(/^[ \t]*(?:module|class)[ \t]+([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_:]*)/)
  m && m[1]
end

# The body of one `def <name>`: its own line down to the next `def` at any
# indentation, or the end of the source. Structure off the masked copy, text
# off the original. `(?![A-Za-z0-9_])` and not `\b`, because a predicate's name
# ends in `?` and `\b` does not fire between `?` and `(`.
def method_body(src, name)
  mask = masked(src)
  at   = mask.index(/^[ \t]*def #{Regexp.escape(name)}(?![A-Za-z0-9_])/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  stop = mask.index(/^[ \t]*def /, at + 1) || mask.length
  src[at...stop]
end

# `CreateOrderOperation` -> `create_order_operation.rb`: Rails' own autoload
# mapping, which is why the file is there and not somewhere a list would have
# to remember.
def operation_file(class_name)
  "#{class_name.gsub(/([a-z0-9])([A-Z])/, '\1_\2').downcase}.rb"
end

# Every `<GuardModule>.<fn>` a verb takes, DIRECTLY in its handler body and
# through the Operation that body hands its arguments to. Both paths, because
# this origin uses both: the read verb calls the guards itself, the write verbs
# delegate first.
def verb_wire_guards(controller_src, operations, guard_module)
  mask = masked(controller_src)
  out  = {}
  declared_verbs(controller_src).each do |verb|
    range = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    next if range.nil?

    body  = mask[range]
    calls = body.scan(/\b#{Regexp.escape(guard_module)}\.([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[?!]?)/).flatten
    if (m = body.match(/\b([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\.call\b/)) && (op = operations[operation_file(m[1])])
      calls += masked(op).scan(/\b#{Regexp.escape(guard_module)}\.([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[?!]?)/).flatten
    end
    out[verb] = calls.uniq unless calls.empty?
  end
  out
end

# Every local a body binds to `expr_re`, so the same fact spelled through a
# local is still the same fact.
def bound_locals(body, expr_re)
  body.scan(/^[ \t]*([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)[ \t]*=[ \t]*(?:#{expr_re})/).flatten.uniq
end

# `expr_re`, or any local this body binds to it, as one alternation.
def operand_regex(body, expr_re)
  ([expr_re] + bound_locals(body, expr_re).map { |l| "#{Regexp.escape(l)}(?![A-Za-z0-9_])" }).join("|")
end

COMPARISON = '(?:<=|<|>=|>)'

# Is `expr_re` an operand of SOME comparison in this body?
def compared_at_all?(body, expr_re)
  term = operand_regex(body, expr_re)
  body.match?(/(?:#{term})[ \t]*#{COMPARISON}/) || body.match?(/#{COMPARISON}[ \t]*(?:#{term})/)
end

# Are `left_re` and `right_re` the two operands of one comparison, either way
# round?
def compared_with?(body, left_re, right_re)
  l = operand_regex(body, left_re)
  r = operand_regex(body, right_re)
  body.match?(/(?:#{l})[ \t]*#{COMPARISON}[ \t]*(?:#{r})/) ||
    body.match?(/(?:#{r})[ \t]*#{COMPARISON}[ \t]*(?:#{l})/)
end

# The whole chain, or the reason there is none.
#
# @return [Array(Hash, String, nil), Array(nil, nil, String)]
def derived_window_guards(sources)
  guard_mod = declared_module(sources[:wire_arguments])
  slot_mod  = declared_module(sources[:delivery_slots])
  return [nil, nil, "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS} declares no module"] if guard_mod.nil?
  return [nil, nil, "#{GETGROCERY_DELIVERY_SLOTS} declares no module"] if slot_mod.nil?

  # THE CLOCK READER, from the slot module's own signatures: a keyword
  # parameter whose DEFAULT names a nullary method of the same module is that
  # module saying «this is where I read now from».
  slots_mask = masked(sources[:delivery_slots])
  # `\b` before the parameter name is load-bearing: without it the greedy
  # `[^)\n]*` stops one character INSIDE the name (`at:` reads as `t:`), which
  # still captures the right default here and would not on the next signature.
  clock = slots_mask.scan(/^[ \t]*def [a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_?!]*\([^)\n]*\b[a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:[ \t]*([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)[,)]/)
                    .flatten.uniq
  return [nil, nil, "no method of #{slot_mod} defaults a keyword argument to another of its methods, " \
                    "so the clock those guards read cannot be resolved"] if clock.size != 1

  clock = clock.first
  return [nil, nil, "#{slot_mod} names `#{clock}` as a keyword default but declares no `def #{clock}`"] \
    if method_body(sources[:delivery_slots], clock).nil?

  # THE PREDICATE AND THE WINDOW READER: the method of the slot module that
  # compares a call of its own against that keyword argument.
  pred = window = nil
  slots_mask.enum_for(:scan, /^[ \t]*def ([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_?!]*)\([^)\n]*\b([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*):[ \t]*#{Regexp.escape(clock)}[,)]/)
            .each do
    name, kw = Regexp.last_match(1), Regexp.last_match(2)
    body     = method_body(sources[:delivery_slots], name)
    next if body.nil?

    m = masked(body).match(/([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\([^()\n]*\)[ \t]*#{COMPARISON}[ \t]*#{Regexp.escape(kw)}(?![A-Za-z0-9_])/)
    next if m.nil?

    pred, window = name, m[1]
    break
  end
  return [nil, nil, "no method of #{slot_mod} compares one of its own readers against that clock, " \
                    "so the window expression cannot be resolved"] if pred.nil?

  # THE DAY EXPRESSION: the chain the guard module applies to the clock. One
  # spelling across the whole guard module or this says so — two would leave no
  # single fact to hold the page to, exactly as two Rails floors leave none.
  #
  # THE CLOCK READER MAY TAKE AN ARGUMENT, and since the per-resource clock rule
  # it does: «now» is a question about a PLACE, so `now(zone)` is the origin
  # asking it about the address it is answering for. The argument list is
  # skipped rather than compared — what this rule holds is that the page reads
  # the SAME chain off the SAME reader, not that it names the same zone
  # variable, which is a local name and none of this rule's business.
  clock_call = "#{Regexp.escape(slot_mod)}\\.#{Regexp.escape(clock)}(?:\\([^()\\n]*\\))?"
  suffixes = masked(sources[:wire_arguments])
             .scan(/#{clock_call}((?:\.[a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)+)/)
             .flatten.uniq
  return [nil, nil, "#{guard_mod} never reads #{slot_mod}.#{clock}, so there is no day expression " \
                    "to hold the page to"] if suffixes.size != 1

  day    = "#{slot_mod}.#{clock}#{suffixes.first}"
  day_re = "#{clock_call}#{Regexp.escape(suffixes.first)}(?![A-Za-z0-9_])"

  # WHICH VERB TAKES WHICH FACT. A guard that reads the day expression is a DAY
  # refusal; one that reaches the predicate is a WINDOW refusal. A guard that
  # does neither (a uuid shape, a served district, a cart) is not this rule's.
  guards = verb_wire_guards(sources[:storefront], sources[:operations], guard_mod)
           .merge(verb_wire_guards(sources[:orders], sources[:operations], guard_mod)) { |_k, a, b| (a + b).uniq }
  facts = {}
  guards.each do |verb, fns|
    seen = []
    fns.each do |fn|
      body = method_body(sources[:wire_arguments], fn)
      next if body.nil?

      mb = masked(body)
      seen << :day    if mb.match?(/#{day_re}/)
      seen << :window if mb.match?(/#{Regexp.escape(slot_mod)}\.#{Regexp.escape(pred)}(?![A-Za-z0-9_])/)
    end
    facts[verb] = seen.uniq unless seen.empty?
  end
  return [nil, nil, "no verb of #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} / #{GETGROCERY_ORDERS} reaches a #{guard_mod} " \
                    "guard that refuses a stale day or a stale window"] if facts.empty?

  [{ facts: facts, day: day, day_re: day_re, window: "#{slot_mod}.#{window}",
     clock: "#{slot_mod}.#{clock}" },
   "#{GETGROCERY_ORDERS} -> app/operations -> #{guard_mod} -> #{slot_mod}.#{pred} " \
   "(day #{day}, window #{slot_mod}.#{window} vs #{slot_mod}.#{clock})"]
end

# R16 -- every worked verb REFUSES a delivery day that has passed and
# RE-CHECKS the window it is about to book, exactly as the origin does
# (K-1366).
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES: the page's `create_order` parsed `delivery_date` with
# `Date.parse`, computed `slot_at` from it and wrote the row — no past-day check
# and no re-validation of the window — and its `reschedule_delivery` did the
# same. Three lines above, the page's OWN comment beside `example_params` says a
# literal there «would publish a `delivery_date` this action refuses as past»,
# which was a sentence about code that refused nothing. getgrocery refuses BOTH
# limbs on BOTH verbs, and its `delivery_slots` hides a window whose start has
# passed — so an operator copying this page shipped a pair of write verbs that
# book a slot their own read verb would never have offered.
#
# NO RULE ABOVE COULD REACH IT, and each for its own reason: WRITE_REQUIRED
# compares required LISTS, PROPERTY_KEYWORDS compares declared KEYWORDS, and a
# «day already gone» is expressible as neither — the domain rolls forward every
# midnight, which is why the origin states it as a handler refusal and not as an
# `enum`. TOTAL_GUARD is the nearest neighbour (a computed value no declared
# property can carry) and its subject is the cart sum.
#
# THE SUBJECT SET IS NOT METHOD-SHAPED, so this rule names no HTTP verb for it
# — AND IT NAMES NO CENSUS EITHER, which is the harder half. The subjects are
# whichever verbs the chain above derives, that set is a property of the
# ORIGIN's guard wiring, and this rule PRINTS it on its own green line with the
# verbs spelled out. Read the line; a count written into this paragraph is a
# copy of a moving fact and goes stale without anything reddening (K-1503).
#
# WHAT THE CHAIN CAN AND CANNOT REACH, because that is the part a reader
# deciding whether this green line covers their change actually needs. It reads
# the BODY of each guard function the controller calls, and it does not follow a
# call from one guard into a sibling. So a refusal one hop away — a guard that
# delegates the day question to another method of the same module — is outside
# the derived set by construction, however real the refusal is. A `get`-routed
# read can therefore be a subject or not for reasons that are about wiring and
# not about routing, which is exactly why calling the subjects «write verbs»
# would be a sentence about this rule that the origin's route table is free to
# refute.
def rule_past_window(page, sources)
  chain, provenance, why = derived_window_guards(sources)
  return vacuous!("PAST_WINDOW", why) if chain.nil?

  ruby     = page_ruby(page)
  mask     = masked(ruby)
  on_page  = declared_verbs(ruby)
  subjects = chain[:facts].select { |verb, _| on_page.include?(verb) }
  if subjects.empty?
    return vacuous!("PAST_WINDOW",
                    "the page declares none of the verb(s) that take a stale-window refusal on this " \
                    "origin (#{chain[:facts].keys.sort.join(", ")})")
  end

  day_re    = chain[:day_re]
  window_re = "#{Regexp.escape(chain[:window])}\\([^()\\n]*\\)"
  clock_re  = "#{Regexp.escape(chain[:clock])}(?![.A-Za-z0-9_])"

  derived = 0
  bad     = []
  subjects.sort.each do |verb, facts|
    range = handler_body_range(mask, verb)
    if range.nil?
      bad << "#{verb} is declared on the page but has no handler body to read"
      next
    end

    body = mask[range]
    facts.each do |fact|
      derived += 1
      case fact
      when :day
        next if compared_at_all?(body, day_re)

        bad << "#{verb} never compares anything against #{chain[:day]}, so it books a delivery day " \
               "that is already gone -- getgrocery refuses it by name"
      when :window
        next if compared_with?(body, window_re, clock_re)

        bad << "#{verb} never compares #{chain[:window]}(...) against #{chain[:clock]}, so it books a " \
               "window that has already begun -- getgrocery re-validates it on this verb"
      end
    end
  end

  check("PAST_WINDOW",
        bad.empty?,
        "#{subjects.size} worked verb(s) (#{subjects.keys.sort.join(", ")}) refuse a stale day " \
        "and a stale window (#{derived} refusal(s) derived)",
        bad.empty? ? provenance : bad.join(" | "))
end

# ── R17's derivation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The monorepo README states publication status CANONICALLY, and the page
# carries a DERIVED COPY of that sentence plus a pointer to where it is stated
# (K-1381). Before this rule the README claimed to be «the one place in either
# published repository that states publication status» while the page stated it
# too, three lines above its own pointer at that section — the two agreed, so no
# reader was misled today, and on publication day a maintainer who believes the
# «one place» sentence changes one file and leaves a live page saying no gem is
# published.
#
# WHAT IS DERIVED: the claim's own words, the section that carries it, and the
# URL of that section — all read out of README.md, none of them typed here. The
# page is held to the WORDS because that is what makes it a copy rather than a
# second statement: reword the canonical sentence and the page is red until it
# carries the new one.

# The `## Heading` a byte offset falls under, and its GitHub anchor slug.
def readme_section_at(readme_src, at)
  head = readme_src[0...at].scan(/^##[ \t]+(.+)$/).flatten.last
  return nil if head.nil?

  [head.strip, head.strip.downcase.gsub(/[^a-z0-9 -]/, "").tr(" ", "-")]
end

# The publication-status claim the README states in the section its canonical
# install line lives in: the first **bolded** run of that section, which is how
# that section opens and what a reader meets first.
#
# @return [Array(String, String, String), nil] claim, heading, anchor
def readme_publication_status(readme_src)
  at = readme_src.index(/^gem[ \t]+"kiosk-all"/)
  return nil if at.nil?

  section = readme_section_at(readme_src, at)
  return nil if section.nil?

  head, anchor = section
  from = readme_src.index(/^##[ \t]+#{Regexp.escape(head)}$/)
  to   = readme_src.index(/^##[ \t]+/, from + 1) || readme_src.length
  m    = readme_src[from...to].match(/\*\*([^*\n]+)\*\*/)
  return nil if m.nil?

  [m[1].strip.sub(/[.!]\z/, ""), head, anchor]
end

# R17 -- the page carries the README's publication-status sentence, and names
# the section it is stated in (K-1381).
def rule_publication_status(page, sources)
  status = readme_publication_status(sources[:readme])
  if status.nil?
    return vacuous!("PUBLICATION_STATUS",
                    "#{MONOREPO_README} states no **bolded** publication-status claim in the section " \
                    "its canonical `gem \"kiosk-all\"` line lives in -- if the gems have shipped and the " \
                    "claim is gone, restate this rule rather than leaving it matching nothing")
  end

  claim, head, anchor = status
  canonical = canonical_install_source(sources[:readme])
  if canonical.nil? || canonical[0] != "github"
    return vacuous!("PUBLICATION_STATUS",
                    "#{MONOREPO_README}'s canonical install line names no `github:` source, so the URL " \
                    "of its «#{head}» section cannot be derived")
  end

  url = "https://github.com/#{canonical[1]}##{anchor}"
  bad = []
  bad << "the page does not carry #{MONOREPO_README}'s claim «#{claim}» -- it is stated canonically " \
         "there and copied here, so a reword there is a reword here" unless page.include?(claim)
  bad << "the page never names #{url}, the section that states it" unless page.include?(url)

  check("PUBLICATION_STATUS",
        bad.empty?,
        "the page carries «#{claim}» and points at #{url}",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{MONOREPO_README} (section «#{head}»)" : bad.join(" | "))
end

# R18 -- the page parses a date argument with the SAME `Date.` constructor
# getgrocery does (K-1406).
#
# WHY THIS IS A DERIVATION AND NOT A BAN, which is the whole point of the rule.
# Two shipped origins take OPPOSITE, both-documented positions on the same
# question. kiosk-demo-hoteling refuses `Date.parse` in so many words -- it
# SCANS rather than validates, so `"x2026-09-01x"` parses and `"Tue"` is
# completed from today's clock -- and pairs a `YYYY-MM-DD` regex with
# `Date.iso8601`. kiosk-demo-getgrocery chooses `Date.parse` deliberately and
# records the choice three times: in `wire_arguments.rb`, in an assertion of
# `wire_arguments_spec.rb`, and in a redteam comment explaining which probes it
# therefore does NOT fire. Neither position is this script's to overturn, and
# a BAN here would overturn one of them by writing it into a gate.
#
# What IS this script's business is that the page is a COPY. It teaches
# getgrocery's verbs, so it must teach getgrocery's date spelling -- whichever
# that becomes. If the divergence is ever resolved toward the strict spelling,
# this rule turns the page red until it follows, which is the drift-catching
# the guide has and the ban would not: a ban would go green on a page that had
# stopped matching its source in the other direction.
#
# Comments are masked on BOTH sides. Every one of these files argues about the
# spelling in prose -- getgrocery's own comment names `Date.iso8601` in order to
# reject it -- so an unmasked scan would compare arguments rather than code.
DATE_CONSTRUCTOR = /\bDate\.(parse|iso8601|strptime|rfc3339|xmlschema|civil|jd|_parse)\b/

def date_constructors(src)
  masked(src).scan(DATE_CONSTRUCTOR).flatten.uniq.sort
end

def rule_date_parser(page, sources)
  demo = (date_constructors(sources[:storefront]) | date_constructors(sources[:wire_arguments])).sort
  if demo.empty?
    return vacuous!("DATE_PARSER",
                    "neither #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} nor #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS} parses a date with a " \
                    "`Date.` constructor, so there is no spelling to derive")
  end

  mine = date_constructors(page_ruby(page))
  return vacuous!("DATE_PARSER", "the page's Ruby parses no date at all") if mine.empty?

  check("DATE_PARSER",
        demo == mine,
        "the page parses dates with #{mine.map { |m| "Date.#{m}" }.join(", ")}",
        demo == mine ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT} + #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS}"
                     : "getgrocery uses #{demo.map { |m| "Date.#{m}" }.join(", ")}; the page uses " \
                       "#{mine.map { |m| "Date.#{m}" }.join(", ")} -- the page is a copy of those verbs and " \
                       "must parse their arguments the way they do")
end

# ── R19..R22: the routes file the page teaches (T-184) ──────────────────────
#
# WHY FOUR RULES AND NOT ONE. The page used to teach a single `mount` line, and
# the sentence above it -- «mounting it draws the entire surface, you write no
# route of your own» -- was TRUE of the shipped engine until T-183 deleted the
# `:kiosk_verb` catch-all pair. It is now four separate facts, and they fail
# independently: WHERE the wire lives (a `draw` split), WHAT draws the protocol
# plane (the mount), WHAT draws the operator's verbs (one explicit line each),
# and the ORDER the two are written in (the mount first, or a verb of the
# operator's can shadow `schema`). One rule would go red on any of them and say
# which only in its detail line; four say it in the name.
#
# Every one derives from getgrocery, the origin the rest of this page is copied
# from -- so the worked routes cannot become the hand-typed table that drifts,
# which is the whole class of defect K-1394 recorded.

# R19 -- the page reaches the wire file the way getgrocery does.
def rule_route_file(page, sources)
  name = sources[:app_routes][DRAW_CALL, 1]
  return vacuous!("ROUTE_FILE", "#{GETGROCERY_APP_ROUTES} makes no `draw(:name)` call, so there is no split to derive") if name.nil?

  wire_rel = GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES[%r{config/routes/[^/]+\.rb\z}]
  return vacuous!("ROUTE_FILE", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} is not a config/routes/<name>.rb file") if wire_rel.nil?
  unless wire_rel == "config/routes/#{name}.rb"
    return vacuous!("ROUTE_FILE",
                    "getgrocery calls draw(:#{name}) but keeps its wire in #{wire_rel}; Rails resolves " \
                    "draw(:x) to config/routes/x.rb, so these two cannot both be right and there is no " \
                    "single fact to hold the page to")
  end

  drawn   = page_ruby(page).match?(/^[ \t]*draw\(:#{Regexp.escape(name)}\)/)
  named   = page.include?(wire_rel)
  missing = []
  missing << "no `draw(:#{name})` line in any Ruby block" unless drawn
  missing << "the page never names #{wire_rel}" unless named
  check("ROUTE_FILE",
        missing.empty?,
        "the page keeps the wire in #{wire_rel} and reaches it with Rails' own draw(:#{name})",
        missing.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_APP_ROUTES} + #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES}" : missing.join("; "))
end

# R20 -- every verb the page declares is drawn with getgrocery's own line.
def rule_verb_routes(page, sources)
  demo = demo_verb_routes(sources)
  return vacuous!("VERB_ROUTES", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} draws no `defaults: { kiosk_verb: … }` route") if demo.empty?

  ruby = page_ruby(page)
  mine = wire_routes(ruby).select { |r| r[:verb] }
  return vacuous!("VERB_ROUTES", "the page draws no verb route at all") if mine.empty?

  owed = declared_verbs(ruby) & demo.keys
  return vacuous!("VERB_ROUTES", "the page declares no verb getgrocery also routes") if owed.empty?

  drawn   = mine.each_with_object({}) { |r, h| h[r[:verb]] = { method: r[:method], path: r[:path], to: r[:to] } }
  dupes   = mine.map { |r| r[:verb] }.tally.select { |_, c| c > 1 }.keys
  missing = owed - drawn.keys
  unknown = drawn.keys - demo.keys
  wrong   = drawn.filter_map do |v, got|
    next if demo[v].nil? || demo[v] == got

    "#{v} is drawn `#{got[:method]} \"#{got[:path]}\" -> #{got[:to]}` where getgrocery draws " \
      "`#{demo[v][:method]} \"#{demo[v][:path]}\" -> #{demo[v][:to]}`"
  end

  problems = []
  problems << "declared but never routed: #{missing.sort.join(", ")}" unless missing.empty?
  problems << "routed but getgrocery routes no such verb: #{unknown.sort.join(", ")}" unless unknown.empty?
  problems << "routed more than once: #{dupes.sort.join(", ")}" unless dupes.empty?
  problems.concat(wrong)
  check("VERB_ROUTES",
        problems.empty?,
        "#{owed.size} verb(s) the page declares (#{owed.sort.join(", ")}) are drawn with getgrocery's own route line",
        problems.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} (#{demo.size} verb route(s) there)" : problems.join("; "))
end

# R21 -- the mount is drawn, and drawn FIRST.
def rule_mount_first(page, sources)
  demo_at   = wire_mount_at(sources[:wire_file])
  demo_line = wire_mount_line(sources[:wire_file])
  demo_rts  = wire_routes(sources[:wire_file])
  return vacuous!("MOUNT_FIRST", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} does not mount Kiosk::Server::Engine") if demo_at.nil?
  return vacuous!("MOUNT_FIRST", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} draws no route for a mount to precede") if demo_rts.empty?
  unless demo_at < demo_rts.map { |r| r[:at] }.min
    return vacuous!("MOUNT_FIRST", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} draws its mount BELOW a route, so there is no ordering to derive")
  end

  ruby     = page_ruby(page)
  mine_rts = wire_routes(ruby)
  return vacuous!("MOUNT_FIRST", "the page draws no route for a mount to precede") if mine_rts.empty?

  mine_at   = wire_mount_at(ruby)
  mine_line = wire_mount_line(ruby)
  first_rt  = mine_rts.map { |r| r[:at] }.min
  problems  = []
  problems << "no Ruby block on the page mounts Kiosk::Server::Engine" if mine_at.nil?
  problems << "the mount is drawn BELOW a route, so an operator verb can shadow the protocol plane" if mine_at && mine_at > first_rt
  problems << "the page mounts `#{mine_line}` where getgrocery mounts `#{demo_line}`" if mine_line && mine_line != demo_line
  check("MOUNT_FIRST",
        problems.empty?,
        "the page draws `#{demo_line}` above all #{mine_rts.size} of its route line(s)",
        problems.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES}" : problems.join("; "))
end

# R22 -- BAN. No route on the page carries a dynamic segment or an unnamed verb.
#
# The subject is derived: getgrocery's wire file draws N routes and not one of
# them is a pattern, so the ban has something live to be a ban ABOUT. If the
# origin itself ever drew one, this says so and holds nothing, rather than
# holding the page to a rule its own source breaks.
def rule_route_pattern(page, sources)
  demo = wire_routes(sources[:wire_file])
  return vacuous!("ROUTE_PATTERN", "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} draws no route at all") if demo.empty?

  loose = demo.select { |r| DYNAMIC_SEGMENT.match?(r[:path]) || r[:verb].nil? }
  unless loose.empty?
    return vacuous!("ROUTE_PATTERN",
                    "#{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} itself draws #{loose.size} pattern route(s) " \
                    "(#{loose.map { |r| r[:path] }.join(", ")}), so this ban has no origin to derive from")
  end

  block = page_wire_block(page)
  return vacuous!("ROUTE_PATTERN", "no Ruby block on the page mounts Kiosk::Server::Engine, so the page shows no wire file") if block.nil?

  mine = wire_routes(block)
  return vacuous!("ROUTE_PATTERN", "the page's wire file draws no route") if mine.empty?

  bad = mine.select { |r| DYNAMIC_SEGMENT.match?(r[:path]) || r[:verb].nil? }
  check("ROUTE_PATTERN",
        bad.empty?,
        "all #{mine.size} route(s) in the page's wire file name one static path and one verb",
        bad.empty? ? "derived from #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES} (#{demo.size} static, named route(s) there)"
                   : bad.map { |r| "`#{r[:method]} \"#{r[:path]}\"`#{r[:verb] ? "" : " names no kiosk_verb"} " \
                                   "is the catch-all T-183 deleted, spelled on the page" }.join("; "))
end

RULES = [
  [:rule_status_prose,       "STATUS_PROSE",        "pointer",    "the guard one repository over that DOES hold this page's status-beside-a-code sentences, which this script's own disclaimer list says nothing derives"],
  [:rule_handler_names,      "HANDLER_NAMES",       "derivation", "the two handler class names the kiosk:install initializer template teaches"],
  [:rule_slots_required,     "SLOTS_REQUIRED",      "derivation", "getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} input_schema `required:` list"],
  [:rule_slots_properties,   "SLOTS_PROPERTIES",    "derivation", "getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} input_schema property names"],
  [:rule_integer_coercion,   "INTEGER_COERCION",    "ban",        "every parameter getgrocery declares `type: \"integer\"`"],
  [:rule_frozen_date,        "FROZEN_DATE",         "ban",        "a YYYY-MM-DD literal, which the verb refuses once it is past"],
  [:rule_getgrocery_handlers, "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS", "derivation", "the handler classes getgrocery's own initializer registers"],
  [:rule_no_require_optional, "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL",    "ban",        "params.require on an argument getgrocery leaves optional"],
  [:rule_rails_floor,        "RAILS_FLOOR",         "derivation", "the Rails MAJOR.MINOR kiosk-server's gemspec declares"],
  [:rule_ruby_floor,         "RUBY_FLOOR",          "derivation", "the Ruby floor kiosk-core's gemspec declares"],
  [:rule_install_source,     "INSTALL_SOURCE",      "derivation", "the install source qualifier the monorepo README declares canonical"],
  [:rule_write_required,     "WRITE_REQUIRED",      "derivation", "the input_schema `required:` list of every verb both sides declare"],
  [:rule_item_shape,         "ITEM_SHAPE",          "ban",        "indexing an element of a declared array-of-objects without checking it is a Hash"],
  [:rule_error_redaction,    "ERROR_REDACTION",     "derivation", "what the rescue seam sends INSTEAD of the exception's own message, and the class it re-raises untouched"],
  [:rule_property_keywords,  "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS",   "derivation", "the JSON Schema assertion keywords getgrocery declares on every property both sides declare, array ELEMENTS included, each Ruby constant resolved to the literal it is assigned"],
  [:rule_total_guard,        "TOTAL_GUARD",         "derivation", "the ceiling getgrocery refuses an unpriceable cart at, walked from the summed total in its create operation to the constant the guard bounds it by -- a COMPUTED value no declared-property rule can reach"],
  [:rule_past_window,        "PAST_WINDOW",         "derivation", "which verbs refuse a delivery day that has already gone and re-check the window they book, walked from each verb's Operation through the wire-argument guards to the clock reader those guards compare against"],
  [:rule_publication_status, "PUBLICATION_STATUS",  "derivation", "the publication-status claim the monorepo README states canonically, and the URL of the section that states it"],
  [:rule_date_parser,        "DATE_PARSER",         "derivation", "the `Date.` constructor getgrocery's read controller and wire-argument guards parse a date argument with"],
  [:rule_route_file,         "ROUTE_FILE",         "derivation", "the `draw(:name)` split getgrocery reaches its wire file by, and that file's own path"],
  [:rule_verb_routes,        "VERB_ROUTES",        "derivation", "getgrocery's own route line for every verb the page declares -- method, path, and the kiosk_verb it defaults"],
  [:rule_mount_first,        "MOUNT_FIRST",        "derivation", "the engine mount getgrocery draws, and the fact it is drawn above every verb route"],
  [:rule_route_pattern,      "ROUTE_PATTERN",      "ban",        "a dynamic path segment, or a route naming no verb -- the catch-all pair T-183 deleted"],
].freeze

def load_sources
  { generator:       repo_read(GENERATOR_INITIALIZER),
    storefront:      repo_read(GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT),
    orders:          repo_read(GETGROCERY_ORDERS),
    getgrocery_init: repo_read(GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER),
uuid_check:      repo_read(CORE_UUID_CHECK),
wire_arguments:  repo_read(GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS),
create_order_op: repo_read(GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER),
delivery_slots:  repo_read(GETGROCERY_DELIVERY_SLOTS),
operations:      repo_read_dir(GETGROCERY_OPERATIONS_DIR),
app_routes:      repo_read(GETGROCERY_APP_ROUTES),
wire_file:       repo_read(GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES),
    problem_schema:  repo_read(PROBLEM_SCHEMA_MIRROR),
    server_gemspec:  repo_read(SERVER_GEMSPEC),
    core_gemspec:    repo_read(CORE_GEMSPEC),
    readme:          repo_read(MONOREPO_README),
    handler_mixin:   repo_read(SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN),
    server_errors:   repo_read(SERVER_ERRORS) }
end

def run_rules(page, sources)
  RULES.each { |m, _n, _k, _d| send(m, page, sources) }
end

# ── --list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def run_list
  puts "#{RULES.size} rules, each deriving from this repository:"
  RULES.each do |_m, name, kind, what|
    puts format("  %-22s %-11s %s", name, kind, what)
  end
  puts
  puts "Derivation sources:"
  [GENERATOR_INITIALIZER, GETGROCERY_STOREFRONT, GETGROCERY_ORDERS, GETGROCERY_INITIALIZER,
   CORE_UUID_CHECK, GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS, GETGROCERY_CREATE_ORDER,
   GETGROCERY_DELIVERY_SLOTS, GETGROCERY_OPERATIONS_DIR,
   GETGROCERY_APP_ROUTES, GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES,
   SERVER_GEMSPEC, CORE_GEMSPEC, MONOREPO_README, SERVER_HANDLER_MIXIN, SERVER_ERRORS].each do |rel|
    path = File.join(ROOT, rel)
    puts "  #{File.exist?(path) ? "present" : "MISSING"}  #{rel}"
  end
  0
end

# ── --self-test ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each arm plants ONE faithful break -- the shape a ledger row actually
# recorded, never a token nobody would type -- and asserts the rule goes red on
# it. Nothing on disk is touched: every plant is a string transformation of an
# in-memory copy. A VACUITY arm per rule blanks the DERIVATION side instead, so
# a rule that has stopped being able to compute its own subject fails rather
# than passing.
#
# IT RUNS WITH NO SIBLING AND NO NETWORK, which is the whole reason it can be
# the push-blocking half of the CI job. When no page resolves it plants into a
# FIXTURE built from these same derivations -- so the fixture cannot rot, and
# the run says in its first line which of the two it used. The derivation side
# is always the real checkout, in both cases.
#
# AND THE REASON IS THAT AND NOT A TOKEN COST -- the correction matters because
# the next wave to weigh «should this leg just check the sibling out?» will meet
# whichever reason is written down (K-1385). A sibling checkout here would cost
# NO cross-repo credential: MEASURED via `gh api`, both `kiosk-hq/kiosk.tech` and
# `kiosk-hq/kiosk` answer `private: false, visibility: public`, and the
# `published-bytes` job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` already checks that same
# sibling out with no token at all. What a sibling checkout would cost is the
# property this leg exists for: a guard that can only be proven where the
# sibling happens to be present is a guard that reads green in every hand-run
# and is red on the push, which is exactly what happened here once. So the
# fixture stays because the leg must be able to go red with NOTHING beside it,
# not because reaching the sibling is expensive.
#
# THE PLANTS ARE DERIVED, not typed. A plant table naming `date` and
# `delivery_address` would be a hand-kept copy of the very facts these rules
# exist to stop being hand-kept: rename a parameter in getgrocery and the arm
# would quietly stop matching, go green, and read as coverage.

def self_test_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)
  FAILURES.clear
  quiet { run_rules(page, sources) }
  hit = FAILURES.any? { |f| f.start_with?("#{expect_rule}:") }
  puts format("  %s  %-48s %s", hit ? "red " : "GREEN", label, hit ? "-> #{expect_rule}" : "!! #{expect_rule} stayed green")
  hit
end

def quiet
  old = $stdout
  $stdout = File.open(File::NULL, "w")
  yield
ensure
  $stdout.close
  $stdout = old
end

# A plant confined to ONE verb's own region of the RAW page: its `def` line to
# the next `def`. R16's defect was one verb losing a refusal its SIBLING still
# carried, so a page-wide `gsub` would go red for the wrong reason and would
# never exercise the per-verb scoping the rule is written with -- a rule that
# only asked «does this page mention the clock anywhere» stays GREEN on the
# plant below, which is the whole point of aiming it at one region.
def plant_in_verb(page, verb)
  at = page.index(/^[ \t]*def #{Regexp.escape(verb)}[ \t]*$/)
  return page if at.nil?

  stop = page.index(/^[ \t]*def /, at + 1) || page.length
  page[0...at] + yield(page[at...stop]) + page[stop..]
end

# ── THE NETWORK READER'S OWN ARMS (K-1367) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY OTHER ARM IN THIS FILE IS FILE-BACKED, and that is exactly why the
# `--network` crash lived as long as it did: a file read off disk comes back
# tagged UTF-8, a socket read comes back tagged ASCII-8BIT, and nothing can
# tell the two apart until a needle carries a non-ASCII byte -- at which point
# `String#include?` raises `Encoding::CompatibilityError` rather than answering.
# It aborted `run_rules` at rule 13 of 15, unconditionally, on every `--network`
# run there has ever been.
#
# So these arms drive `resolve_page` through the REAL network branch, with the
# response `Net::HTTP` actually hands back, and assert BOTH halves: that what
# comes out is text every rule can compare, and that a break planted in those
# BYTES is still caught. The second half is what stops the repair degenerating
# into «return something that never raises» -- an empty string satisfies the
# first half and holds nothing.
#
# No socket is opened: `Net::HTTP.get_response` is aliased away for the
# duration and put back afterwards. `KIOSK_TECH_DIR` IS LEFT EXACTLY AS THE
# CALLER SET IT (K-1388): this helper used to unset it, because the variable was
# resolved BEFORE `--network` and the CI leg sets it -- which meant the arms
# below never exercised the precedence at all, and a regression of that
# shadowing would have been invisible here. `--network` now outranks it, and one
# of the arms sets it on purpose to prove so.
def with_stubbed_response(bytes, charset: "utf-8")
  res = Net::HTTPOK.new("1.1", "200", "OK")
  res["content-type"] = "text/html#{charset ? "; charset=#{charset}" : ""}"
  res.instance_variable_set(:@body, bytes)
  res.instance_variable_set(:@read, true)
  Net::HTTP.singleton_class.send(:alias_method, :kiosk_self_test_get_response, :get_response)
  Net::HTTP.define_singleton_method(:get_response) { |*_a, **_k| res }
  yield
ensure
  Net::HTTP.singleton_class.send(:alias_method, :get_response, :kiosk_self_test_get_response)
  Net::HTTP.singleton_class.send(:remove_method, :kiosk_self_test_get_response)
end

# What `--network` gets back for a given set of bytes, without a network.
def network_resolved(bytes)
  out = nil
  with_stubbed_response(bytes) { out = resolve_page(explicit: nil, network: true) }
  out
end

def network_arm(label, ok, detail)
  puts format("  %s  %-48s %s", ok ? "ok  " : "FAIL", label, detail)
  ok
end

# ── EVERY ARM DECLARES ITS OWN KIND (K-1389) ─────────────────────────────────
#
# The summary used to derive its planted-versus-vacuity split by SUBTRACTING two
# totals -- `arms.size - RULES.size` planted, `RULES.size` vacuity -- which
# assumes exactly one vacuity arm per rule and every other arm planted. Both
# halves of that assumption are false and were already false when it was
# written: PROPERTY_KEYWORDS carries TWO vacuity arms, and the network reader's
# arms are neither kind. MEASURED at the fix: 25 planted, 18 vacuity, 4 network
# = 47, against a printed «30 planted breaks, 17 vacuity». The verdict was never
# wrong -- it counts the failures, not the split -- but a guard that derives a
# self-description WRONGLY is the K-1340 class exactly, and the error grows with
# every arm that is not one of the two assumed kinds.
#
# So the kind is DECLARED by the helper the call site uses, and the summary
# COUNTS. Three kinds, and the names say what question the arm answers:
#
#   :plant    a break planted in the PAGE, asserting a rule still reddens.
#   :vacuity  the DERIVATION side blanked, asserting the rule says it is holding
#             nothing rather than passing over an empty subject.
#   :network  the `--network` reader itself -- its decoding, its byte
#             comparison, and its precedence over the environment.
ARM_KINDS = %i[plant vacuity network].freeze

def plant_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)
  [:plant, self_test_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)]
end

def vacuity_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)
  [:vacuity, self_test_arm(label, page, sources, expect_rule)]
end

def net_arm(label, ok, detail)
  [:network, network_arm(label, ok, detail)]
end

# Every value the plants and the fixture are built from, read out of the repo.
def derived_facts(sources)
  handlers = handler_names(sources[:generator]).first
  req, props = verb_input_schema(sources[:storefront], WORKED_VERB)
  ints = (integer_param_names(sources[:storefront]) | integer_param_names(sources[:orders])).to_a.sort
  { handlers: handlers,
    required: req,
    properties: props,
    optional: props - req,
    integers: ints,
    # The write-surface facts. `orders_schemas` is every verb getgrocery's
    # orders controller declares, with its top-level (required, properties)
    # pair; `arrays` is every input parameter declared as an array of objects.
    # Both are derived, so a rename in getgrocery reddens an arm rather than
    # quietly emptying it.
    orders_schemas: declared_verbs(sources[:orders]).filter_map { |v|
      sch = verb_input_schema(sources[:orders], v)
      [v, sch] if sch && !sch[0].nil? && !sch[1].nil?
    }.to_h,
    arrays: (array_of_object_param_names(sources[:storefront]) |
             array_of_object_param_names(sources[:orders])).to_a.sort,
    getgrocery: handler_names(sources[:getgrocery_init]).first,
    # K-1341: the rescue seam itself, and the sentences it sends in place of
    # the exception's own. Both derived, so a reworded hint or a renamed class
    # reddens the arm rather than leaving it matching a string nobody sends.
    seam:  rescue_seam_body(sources[:handler_mixin]),
    hints: rescued_hints(sources[:server_errors]),
    # K-1359: the ceiling the fixture's summing verb guards with, and the value
    # the drift plant is built against. Derived, so a renamed link in the origin's
    # chain empties this and the self-test SAYS so instead of building a fixture
    # that agrees with nothing.
    ceiling: derived_total_ceiling(sources).first,
    # K-1411: the `Date.` constructors this origin parses a caller's date with.
    # DERIVED for R18's own reason -- the fixture used to write `Date.parse` as a
    # literal, so the day getgrocery added a second constructor the fixture kept
    # teaching one and the CI leg (which is the fixture leg) went red on a page
    # nobody had touched. A hand-kept copy of the fact a rule exists to derive is
    # the one thing this file's header forbids, and it was sitting in the fixture.
    date_ctors: (date_constructors(sources[:storefront]) |
                 date_constructors(sources[:wire_arguments])).sort,
    # K-1366: which verbs owe a stale-day / stale-window refusal, and the three
    # expressions they owe it in. Derived, so the fixture below writes the
    # origin's own spelling and the plants aim at text R16 actually reads.
    window: derived_window_guards(sources).first,
    # K-1381: the publication-status claim the README states canonically, its
    # section and that section's anchor -- the fixture carries the derived copy
    # R17 holds the page to.
    # One (code, status) binding out of the schema mirror, so the fixture page
    # can carry the SHAPE STATUS_PROSE reads without a typed sentence (K-1519).
    problem_pair: problem_pairs(sources[:problem_schema].to_s).first,
    publication: readme_publication_status(sources[:readme]),
    # K-1360: every verb's property ASSERTION KEYWORDS, array elements
    # included, and the constant table they resolve through. The fixture writes
    # these into the page it builds, which is what makes the fixture mode a
    # real page rather than a page R14 is red on -- see `fixture_keywords`.
    assertions: (declared_verbs(sources[:storefront]) | declared_verbs(sources[:orders]))
                .to_h { |v| [v, demo_property_assertions(sources, v) || {}] },
    consts: demo_constants(sources),
    rails: rails_floor(sources[:server_gemspec]),
    ruby: ruby_floor(sources[:core_gemspec]),
    install: canonical_install_source(sources[:readme]),
    # T-184: the routes file, on the derivation side. `routes` is getgrocery's
    # own line per verb, `mount` the mount line it draws above them, `draw` the
    # split name its config/routes.rb reaches the file by, and `wire_rel` the
    # path that name resolves to. All four are read, never typed, for the reason
    # the fixture's date constructors were (K-1411): a literal copy here is a
    # hand-kept duplicate of the very fact R19..R22 exist to derive.
    routes: demo_verb_routes(sources),
    mount: wire_mount_line(sources[:wire_file]),
    draw: sources[:app_routes][DRAW_CALL, 1],
    wire_rel: GETGROCERY_WIRE_ROUTES[%r{config/routes/[^/]+\.rb\z}] }
end

# ── THE FIXTURE CARRIES getgrocery's KEYWORDS (K-1360) ───────────────────────
#
# A fixture that wrote every property as a bare `{ type: "string" }` is not "a
# page that satisfies every rule by construction" -- it is a page R14 is RED
# on, because getgrocery declares `format`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `minItems`
# and `pattern` that such a page omits. Worse, it is a page the K-1344/K-1354
# plants cannot aim at at all: their coordinates are derived from keywords BOTH
# sides declare, so a keywordless fixture leaves `under` and `elem` nil and the
# whole self-test aborts before its first arm.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-07, in exactly the environment `.github/workflows/ci.yml`
# runs this leg in: `KIOSK_TECH_DIR=/nonexistent bin/check-onboarding-derivation
# --self-test` exited 1 at the plant-derivation guard. So the PUSH-BLOCKING half
# of the CI job could only ever be red, and it read green everywhere it was run
# by hand because a workspace checkout HAS the sibling. The remedy is not to let
# the fixture mode skip those arms -- a leg that passes by covering less is the
# failure this guard exists to detect -- it is to make the fixture carry what
# the rules compare, which is what the header above has always claimed of it.
#
# Every keyword is READ OUT OF getgrocery, never typed, and every Ruby constant
# is RESOLVED to the literal it is assigned: the page's standalone controller
# can name no constant of the demo's, so the literal is the only thing it can
# honestly write, and that is the same resolution R14 performs on the other side
# of the comparison. A constant that resolves to nothing is fatal HERE too --
# building a fixture that silently dropped the keyword would hand R14 two copies
# of a hole instead of a comparison.
def fixture_keywords(assertions, consts, where)
  assertions.map do |kw, value|
    literal = constant_reference?(value) ? consts[value] : value
    if literal.nil?
      warn "self-test cannot build its fixture: #{where} declares #{kw}: #{value}, which resolves " \
           "to no literal in #{CORE_UUID_CHECK} or #{GETGROCERY_WIRE_ARGUMENTS}"
      exit 1
    end
    ", #{kw}: #{literal}"
  end.join
end

# One `properties:` block as the fixture writes it. A property getgrocery
# declares as an ARRAY of objects additionally carries its ELEMENT schema under
# `items:` -- the depth R14 was blind at until K-1354, and the depth the `elem`
# plant aims at. The element's own keys come from the same derived hash, keyed
# `<name>[].<sub>`, so nothing here is a second hand-typed copy of the origin.
#
# EVERY KEYWORD ENDS IN A COMMA, including the last one before a closing brace,
# and that is load-bearing rather than a style choice: `assertion_value` reads a
# non-bracketed value to the comma OR THE END OF THE LINE, so a keyword written
# last on its line comes back as `"date" }` -- the closing brace included -- and
# compares unequal to getgrocery's `"date"`. The origin never hits this because
# its declarations are multi-line; a fixture that writes one property per line
# does, and the first writing of this function did.
def fixture_property_lines(names, assertions, consts, indent)
  names.map do |n|
    own   = assertions[n] || {}
    elems = assertions.select { |k, _| k.start_with?("#{n}[].") }
    next "#{indent}#{n}: { type: \"string\"#{fixture_keywords(own, consts, n)}, }," if elems.empty?

    inner = elems.map { |k, kws|
      "#{k.split("[].").last}: { type: \"string\"#{fixture_keywords(kws, consts, k)}, }"
    }.join(", ")
    "#{indent}#{n}: { type: \"array\"#{fixture_keywords(own, consts, n)}, " \
      "items: { type: \"object\", properties: { #{inner} } }, },"
  end.join("\n")
end

# R16's pair of refusals as the fixture writes them: the DAY compared against
# the day expression, and the WINDOW compared against the clock, both in the
# spelling `derived_window_guards` resolved out of getgrocery. `&lt;` and
# `&gt;` because the fixture is HTML the same `page_ruby` decodes — a raw `<`
# here would not survive the round trip the real page takes.
def fixture_window_guard(f)
  w = f[:window]
  return "" if w.nil?

  ["    return if requested_day &lt; #{w[:day]}",
   "    slot_start = #{w[:window]}(requested_day, 1)",
   "    return if slot_start &lt;= #{w[:clock]}"].join("\n")
end

# THE FIXTURE'S ROUTES FILE, DERIVED LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE IN IT (T-184).
#
# Two blocks, because the page teaches two: `config/routes.rb` reaching the
# split with Rails' own `draw`, and the wire file itself -- the mount first,
# then one line per verb THIS FIXTURE declares. The verb list is intersected
# with getgrocery's routed verbs rather than assumed equal to it, so a demo
# that stops routing a verb empties the arm loudly (VERB_ROUTES goes red on the
# unplanted run) instead of the fixture quietly writing a line nobody drew.
def fixture_app_routes(f)
  "# config/routes.rb\nRails.application.routes.draw do\n  draw(:#{f[:draw]})\nend\n"
end

def fixture_wire_file(f)
  verbs = (([WORKED_VERB] + f[:orders_schemas].keys) & f[:routes].keys).sort
  lines = verbs.map { |v|
    r = f[:routes][v]
    %(#{r[:method]} "#{r[:path]}", to: "#{r[:to]}", defaults: { kiosk_verb: "#{v}" })
  }
  "# #{f[:wire_rel]}\n#{f[:mount]}\n\n#{lines.join("\n")}\n"
end

# A minimal page that satisfies every rule by construction, so the arms
# below are testing the RULES rather than the fixture.
def fixture_page(f)
  ruby_init = "Kiosk.configure do |c|\n  c.handlers = %w[#{f[:handlers].join(" ")}]\nend\n"
  props     = fixture_property_lines(f[:properties], f[:assertions][WORKED_VERB] || {}, f[:consts],
                                     " " * 17)
  reads     = f[:integers].map { |n| "    #{n} = whole_number(params[:#{n}])" }.join("\n")
  # R16's two refusals, in the origin's own spelling (K-1366). Every fixture
  # handler carries them because the SUBJECT list is derived at run time -- a
  # fixture that guessed which verbs owe them would make the rule vacuous for
  # the rest, which is the "passes by covering less" shape the fixture exists
  # to avoid.
  window    = fixture_window_guard(f)
  # One line per constructor the origin uses, derived rather than typed: R18
  # compares the SET of `Date.` constructors on the two sides, so the fixture has
  # to name every one getgrocery names or it teaches a narrower page than the
  # origin has (K-1411).
  date_reads = f[:date_ctors]
               .map { |c| "    Date.#{c}(params[:#{f[:optional].first}].to_s)" }
               .join("\n")
  ruby_verb = <<~RUBY
    class #{f[:handlers].first} &lt; ApplicationController
      include Kiosk::Handler

      kind :query
      input_schema type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
                   properties: {
    #{props}
                   },
                   required: [#{f[:required].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]
      example_params({})
      def #{WORKED_VERB}
    #{reads}
    #{window}
    #{date_reads}
      end
    end
  RUBY
  key, value = f[:install]
  gemfile = %(# Gemfile\ngem "kiosk-all", #{key}: "#{value}"\n)
  # R13's two halves, both derived (K-1360). Without them the fixture is a page
  # ERROR_REDACTION is red on WHATEVER is planted into it, so its two arms would
  # have observed a rule that was already failing and read that as coverage --
  # the same "passes by covering less" shape the fixture mode exists to avoid.
  errors_ns = reraised_class(f[:seam]).to_s.sub(/::Base\z/, "")
  hint      = f[:hints].values.first
  # R17's derived copy (K-1381): the README's own claim, and the URL of the
  # section that states it. Both read out of README.md, so a reword there makes
  # the fixture carry the new sentence rather than a stale one.
  status_url = "https://github.com/#{f[:install][1]}##{f[:publication][2]}"
  # The one PROSE shape a rule reads (K-1519): a status beside a vocabulary
  # code, in the binding the schema mirror declares. Derived, so a rebinding
  # upstream moves this sentence rather than leaving the fixture teaching a
  # pair the schema no longer holds.
  status_prose = f[:problem_pair] ?
    %(<p>Without one it refuses with a <code>#{f[:problem_pair][1]} #{f[:problem_pair][0]}</code> problem document.</p>) : ""
  [status_prose,
   %(<p>getgrocery registers #{(f[:getgrocery] - f[:handlers]).join(", ")} instead.</p>),
   %(<p>Needs Rails #{f[:rails]} and Ruby #{f[:ruby]}.</p>),
   %(<p>#{f[:publication][0]}. Publication status is stated once, at <a href="#{status_url}">#{status_url}</a>.</p>),
   %(<p>Raise what you would raise anyway: the caller is sent "#{hint}" in place of your
      exception's own sentence, and #{errors_ns} is the route you speak deliberately through.</p>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{gemfile}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{ruby_init}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{ruby_verb}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{fixture_write_surface(f)}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{fixture_app_routes(f)}</code></pre>),
   %(<pre><code class="language-ruby">#{fixture_wire_file(f)}</code></pre>)].join("\n")
end

# The write half of the fixture: one declaration per verb getgrocery's orders
# controller declares, carrying that verb's own required list and parameters.
# WRITE_REQUIRED and ITEM_SHAPE have nothing to hold without it -- and a fixture
# that made them VACUOUS would fail the unplanted arm rather than pass it, which
# is the point of the vacuity tier.
def fixture_write_surface(f)
  blocks = f[:orders_schemas].map do |verb, (req, props)|
    prop_lines = fixture_property_lines(props, f[:assertions][verb] || {}, f[:consts], " " * 17)
    # A verb that declares an array-of-objects parameter reads it the way the
    # ban requires: element shape checked before anything indexes it.
    # ... and totals it the way R15 requires: bound to a local, refused at the
    # derived ceiling, and the refusal is the first thing to touch the local.
    reads = (props & f[:arrays]).map { |n|
      "    return if params[:#{n}].is_a?(Array) && params[:#{n}].all? { |item| item.is_a?(Hash) }\n" \
      "    cart_total = params[:#{n}].sum { |item| item[:qty] }\n" \
      "    return if cart_total &gt; #{f[:ceiling]}"
    }.join("\n")
    <<~VERB
      kind :action
        input_schema type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
                     properties: {
      #{prop_lines}
                     },
                     required: [#{req.map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]
        example_params({})
        def #{verb}
      #{reads}
      #{fixture_window_guard(f)}
        end
    VERB
  end
  "class #{f[:handlers].last} &lt; ApplicationController\n  include Kiosk::Handler\n\n  " +
    blocks.join("\n  ") + "end\n"
end

def run_self_test
  sources = load_sources
  f       = derived_facts(sources)
  if f[:handlers].nil? || f[:required].nil? || f[:properties].nil? || f[:optional].empty? ||
     f[:integers].empty? || f[:rails].nil? || f[:ruby].nil? || f[:install].nil? ||
     f[:orders_schemas].empty? || f[:arrays].empty? || f[:seam].nil? || f[:hints].empty? ||
     f[:ceiling].nil? || f[:window].nil? || f[:publication].nil? ||
     f[:routes].empty? || f[:mount].nil? || f[:draw].nil? || f[:wire_rel].nil?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its own plants from this checkout: #{f.inspect}"
    return 1
  end

  page, provenance, = resolve_page(explicit: nil, network: false)
  real = !page.nil?
  page ||= fixture_page(f)
  puts "page side: #{real ? provenance : "BUILT-IN FIXTURE (no page resolved; derivation side is still this checkout)"}"
  puts

  int      = f[:integers].include?("delivery_slot_id") ? "delivery_slot_id" : f[:integers].first
  opt      = f[:optional].first
  req_lit  = "required: [#{f[:required].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]"
  divergent = (f[:getgrocery] - f[:handlers]).first

  # The WRITE_REQUIRED plant needs a required name that belongs to exactly ONE
  # verb, so the substitution below cannot land on another verb's list. Derived
  # by counting names across every required list this checkout declares --
  # never typed, because a typed "items" would go on matching a verb that had
  # been renamed and the arm would pass while covering nothing.
  all_required = f[:orders_schemas].values.map(&:first) + [f[:required]]
  write_anchor = f[:orders_schemas].values.map(&:first).flatten
                                   .find { |n| all_required.count { |l| l.include?(n) } == 1 }
  array_param  = f[:arrays].first

  # K-1341's two halves, both read off the live engine.
  errors_ns      = reraised_class(f[:seam]).to_s.sub(/::Base\z/, "")
  redaction_hint = f[:hints].values.find { |h| page.include?(h) } || f[:hints].values.first

  # K-1344's plant coordinates, derived from the live pair rather than typed:
  #   KW_OVER   a property BOTH sides declare on which getgrocery asserts
  #             nothing -- the only place a page-side keyword is unambiguously
  #             one getgrocery does not have.
  #   KW_VALUE  a keyword and literal value getgrocery uses SOMEWHERE, so the
  #             plant is a real keyword of this schema family and not a token.
  #   KW_UNDER  a literal-valued keyword both sides carry, for the direction a
  #             one-sided rule would miss.
  #   KW_ELEM   the same, one level down, inside an array's ELEMENT schema --
  #             the depth this rule could not see at all until K-1354, so an arm
  #             at the top level alone would have read as coverage of both.
  #   KW_CONST  a keyword getgrocery writes as a Ruby CONSTANT, for the
  #             resolution that replaced K-1344's printed note. Its plant is on
  #             the DERIVATION side and lives in the vacuity section below.
  # KW_OVER and KW_UNDER stay TOP-LEVEL by construction: their plants are written
  # as `<name>: {` against the page, and a nested key is spelled `items[].qty`
  # here and `qty:` there.
  kw_over = kw_under = kw_value = kw_elem = kw_const = nil
  ruby_now = page_ruby(page)
  shared_verbs(page, sources).each do |verb|
    d = demo_property_assertions(sources, verb)
    m = page_property_assertions(ruby_now, verb)
    next if d.nil? || m.nil?

    d.each_value { |as| as.each { |k, v| kw_value ||= [k, v] if literal_assertion?(v) } }
    (d.keys & m.keys).sort.each do |prop|
      nested = prop.include?("[].")
      kw_over ||= [verb, prop] if !nested && d[prop].empty? && m[prop].empty?
      d[prop].each do |kw, v|
        kw_under ||= [verb, prop, kw, v] if !nested && literal_assertion?(v) && m[prop][kw] == v
        kw_elem  ||= [verb, prop, kw, m[prop][kw]] if nested && m[prop].key?(kw)
        kw_const ||= [verb, prop, kw, v] if constant_reference?(v)
      end
    end
  end
  if errors_ns.empty? || redaction_hint.nil? || kw_over.nil? || kw_under.nil? || kw_value.nil? ||
     kw_elem.nil? || kw_const.nil?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its K-1341/K-1344/K-1354 plants: " \
         "errors_ns=#{errors_ns.inspect} hint=#{redaction_hint.inspect} " \
         "over=#{kw_over.inspect} under=#{kw_under.inspect} value=#{kw_value.inspect} " \
         "elem=#{kw_elem.inspect} const=#{kw_const.inspect}"
    return 1
  end

  # A plant that changed nothing proves nothing, and a rule can be red for a
  # reason the arm never planted. So each K-1354 plant is built here and checked
  # to have MOVED the text it aims at before the arm runs.
  planted = lambda do |what, before, after|
    next after unless before == after

    warn "self-test plant is a NO-OP and would prove nothing: #{what}"
    exit 1
  end

  puts "── planted breaks (page side) ──"
  arms = []
  # PLANTED INSIDE THE `c.handlers` LINE, not at the page's first mention of the
  # class (K-1404). HANDLER_NAMES reads `c.handlers = %w[...]` out of the page's
  # RUBY blocks, so a plant that swaps the first textual occurrence anywhere on
  # the page breaks nothing the rule looks at — and the day the page grew a prose
  # paragraph naming the class ahead of the first code block, that is exactly
  # what happened: the arm stayed GREEN with the rule untouched.
  arms << plant_arm("a handler class the generator does not teach",
                        page.sub(/(c\.handlers\s*=\s*%w\[)#{Regexp.escape(f[:handlers].first)}/,
                                 '\1Kiosk::PlantedController'), sources, "HANDLER_NAMES")
  arms << plant_arm("#{WORKED_VERB} requires #{opt.inspect} instead",
                        page.sub(req_lit, "required: [#{opt.inspect}]"), sources, "SLOTS_REQUIRED")
  arms << plant_arm("the #{opt.inspect} parameter renamed away",
                        page.sub(/\b#{Regexp.escape(opt)}:(\s*)\{(\s*)type:/, "#{opt}_planted:\\1{\\2type:"), sources, "SLOTS_PROPERTIES")
  arms << plant_arm("#{int} read with .to_i again",
                        page.sub("whole_number(params[:#{int}])", "params[:#{int}].to_i"), sources, "INTEGER_COERCION")
  arms << plant_arm("a calendar literal frozen into example_params",
                        page.sub("example_params({", 'example_params({ planted: "2026-08-12",'), sources, "FROZEN_DATE")
  # The page silently switched to a date spelling getgrocery does not use.
  # Through DATE_CONSTRUCTOR and not a literal `"Date.parse("`, for the reason the
  # fixture's own copy of that literal gave (K-1411): the page names more than one
  # constructor and moved the one this needle was aimed at, so the plant became a
  # NO-OP the day the origin's spelling changed.
  #
  # AND THE NEEDLE IS DERIVED FOR THE SAME REASON THE HAYSTACK IS. It used to be
  # the typed literal `Date.iso8601` -- the OTHER origin's spelling on the day it
  # was written -- and the day getgrocery converged on that spelling the plant
  # became a no-op again, one line below the paragraph explaining why a typed
  # needle goes stale. What this arm has to swap to is whichever spelling the
  # origin is NOT on, so it is read off the same derivation the rule uses.
  other_ctor = (%w[parse iso8601 strptime] - f[:date_ctors]).first
  arms << plant_arm("the page parses dates with a constructor getgrocery does not use",
                        planted.call("date constructor swapped on the page",
                                     page, page.gsub(DATE_CONSTRUCTOR, "Date.#{other_ctor}")),
                        sources, "DATE_PARSER")
  arms << plant_arm("getgrocery's own #{divergent} un-named",
                        page.gsub(divergent, "the read controller"), sources, "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS")
  arms << plant_arm("params.require(:#{opt}) back in the handler",
                        page.sub("params[:#{opt}]", "params.require(:#{opt})"), sources, "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL")
  # gsub, not sub: the page names each floor more than once, and a plant that
  # leaves one copy standing proves nothing about the rule.
  arms << plant_arm("the page names a Rails line the gemspec excludes",
                        page.gsub("Rails #{f[:rails]}", "Rails 7.1"), sources, "RAILS_FLOOR")
  arms << plant_arm("the page names a Ruby below the declared floor",
                        page.gsub("Ruby #{f[:ruby]}", "Ruby 2.7.0"), sources, "RUBY_FLOOR")
  # sub, not gsub: ONE gem line loses the qualifier while its siblings keep it.
  # That is the half-relink shape -- the one a wave actually ships -- and a gsub
  # plant would never exercise it.
  arms << plant_arm("one gem line loses the canonical source qualifier",
                        page.sub(%(#{f[:install][0]}: "#{f[:install][1]}"), ""), sources, "INSTALL_SOURCE")
  # K-1338's shape: ONE verb's required list gains a name getgrocery does not
  # require. Renaming the anchor inside the list produces both halves at once —
  # a name required here and nowhere in getgrocery, and a name getgrocery
  # requires that the page now declares but no longer requires.
  arms << plant_arm("#{write_anchor.inspect} required under another name",
                        page.sub(/(required:\s*\[[^\]]*)"#{Regexp.escape(write_anchor)}"/) {
                          "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}\"#{write_anchor}_planted\""
                        }, sources, "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  # And the other direction, the one a "page requires too much" rule alone
  # would miss: an argument getgrocery REQUIRES, still declared on the page,
  # demoted to optional.
  arms << plant_arm("#{write_anchor.inspect} demoted to optional on the page",
                        page.sub(/(required:\s*\[[^\]]*)"#{Regexp.escape(write_anchor)}",\s*/) {
                          Regexp.last_match(1)
                        }, sources, "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  # K-1339's shape: the element-shape guard removed from the handler that
  # indexes a declared array of objects.
  arms << plant_arm("the #{array_param} element-shape guard removed",
                        page.gsub("is_a?(Hash)", "respond_to?(:[])"), sources, "ITEM_SHAPE")
  # K-1341's shape, both halves: the page paraphrases the sentence the wire
  # actually sends, and the page stops naming the deliberate route.
  arms << plant_arm("the quoted #{errors_ns.split("::").last} hint paraphrased",
                        page.sub(redaction_hint, "check your arguments and try again."),
                        sources, "ERROR_REDACTION")
  arms << plant_arm("the page stops naming #{errors_ns}",
                        page.gsub(errors_ns, "a Kiosk error"), sources, "ERROR_REDACTION")
  # K-1344's shape: a property BOTH sides declare gains a keyword getgrocery
  # does not, so the page publishes a stricter shape than the origin performs.
  arms << plant_arm("#{kw_over[0]}.#{kw_over[1]} gains #{kw_value[0]}: #{kw_value[1]}",
                        page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(kw_over[1])}:\s*\{\s*type:\s*"[a-z]+")/,
                                 "\\1, #{kw_value[0]}: #{kw_value[1]}"),
                        sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")
  # And the other direction, which a "the page is too strict" rule alone would
  # not reach: a literal keyword getgrocery declares, dropped from the page.
  arms << plant_arm("#{kw_under[0]}.#{kw_under[1]} loses #{kw_under[2]}: #{kw_under[3]}",
                        page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(kw_under[1])}:\s*\{[^}]*?),\s*#{kw_under[2]}:\s*#{Regexp.escape(kw_under[3])}/) {
                          Regexp.last_match(1)
                        }, sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")
  # K-1354's own shape, at the depth that made it invisible rather than merely
  # uncompared: the page's copy of an ARRAY ELEMENT's schema loses a keyword
  # getgrocery declares on it. The page had no ceiling on `create_order`'s `qty`
  # at all, and its handler answers an oversized one with the 500 K-1047 recorded
  # on the origin.
  elem_name = kw_elem[1].split("[].").last
  arms << plant_arm("#{kw_elem[0]}.#{kw_elem[1]} loses #{kw_elem[2]}: #{kw_elem[3]}",
                        planted.call("element keyword #{kw_elem[1]}.#{kw_elem[2]}", page,
                                     page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(elem_name)}:\s*\{[^}]*?),\s*#{kw_elem[2]}:\s*#{Regexp.escape(kw_elem[3])}/) {
                                       Regexp.last_match(1)
                                     }),
                        sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")

  # K-1359's shape, three spellings. The coordinates come from the page itself
  # through the same reader the rule uses, so a plant can never aim at text R15
  # does not look at -- and `planted` refuses a substitution that changed nothing.
  site = page_total_sites(ruby_now).find { |s| s[:local] && s[:token] }
  if site.nil?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its K-1359 plants: the page has no bound-and-guarded summed total"
    return 1
  end
  local   = site[:local]
  token   = site[:token]
  drifted = (f[:ceiling].to_i / 1000).to_s
  # (a) THE SUM IS NOT BOUND -- the pre-fix shape, where the total goes straight
  # into the row and there is nothing for a guard to hold.
  arms << plant_arm("#{site[:verb]}'s summed total no longer bound to `#{local}`",
                        planted.call("summed-total binding", page,
                                     page.sub(/^([ \t]*)#{Regexp.escape(local)}[ \t]*=[ \t]*(\S[^\n]*\.sum)/) {
                                       "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}#{Regexp.last_match(2)}"
                                     }),
                        sources, "TOTAL_GUARD")
  # (b) THE CEILING DRIFTS to an invented basket size, in whichever spelling the
  # page uses -- a constant it assigns, or an inline literal.
  drift = if token.match?(/\A\d/)
            page.sub(/(#{Regexp.escape(local)}\s*(?:&gt;|>|>=|<|<=)\s*)#{Regexp.escape(token)}/) {
              "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}#{drifted}"
            }
          else
            page.sub(/^([ \t]*#{Regexp.escape(token)}[ \t]*=[ \t]*)\S+/) { "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}#{drifted}" }
          end
  arms << plant_arm("the cart ceiling drifts to #{drifted}",
                        planted.call("cart ceiling #{token}", page, drift),
                        sources, "TOTAL_GUARD")
  # (c) THE GUARD IS TOO LATE -- something reads the total before the refusal.
  # A bound-and-guarded total whose guard nothing reaches first is not a guard.
  arms << plant_arm("`#{local}` consumed before the ceiling comparison",
                        planted.call("guard ordering", page,
                                     page.sub(/^([ \t]*#{Regexp.escape(local)}[ \t]*=[^\n]*\.sum[^\n]*\n)/) {
                                       "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}    Rails.logger.info(#{local})\n"
                                     }),
                        sources, "TOTAL_GUARD")

  # K-1366's shape, both limbs, planted in ONE verb's region so the arm also
  # proves the rule is per-verb rather than page-wide: every other handler
  # still carries both refusals while this one loses them.
  window_verb = f[:window][:facts].find { |v, facts| page_ruby(page).match?(/^[ \t]*def #{Regexp.escape(v)}[ \t]*$/) && facts.include?(:window) }
  if window_verb.nil?
    warn "self-test cannot derive its K-1366 plants: no verb of " \
         "#{f[:window][:facts].keys.join(", ")} is declared on the page with a window refusal"
    return 1
  end
  window_verb = window_verb.first
  arms << plant_arm("#{window_verb} stops refusing a day that is already gone",
                        planted.call("day refusal in #{window_verb}", page,
                                     plant_in_verb(page, window_verb) { |r| r.gsub(f[:window][:day], "#{f[:window][:day]}_planted") }),
                        sources, "PAST_WINDOW")
  arms << plant_arm("#{window_verb} stops re-checking the window it books",
                        planted.call("window refusal in #{window_verb}", page,
                                     plant_in_verb(page, window_verb) { |r| r.gsub("#{f[:window][:window]}(", "#{f[:window][:window]}_planted(") }),
                        sources, "PAST_WINDOW")
  # K-1381's shape, both halves: the page stops carrying the README's claim,
  # and the page stops pointing at the section that states it.
  status_url = "https://github.com/#{f[:install][1]}##{f[:publication][2]}"
  arms << plant_arm("the page drops the README's publication-status claim",
                        planted.call("publication claim", page,
                                     page.gsub(f[:publication][0], "Kiosk gems install from git")),
                        sources, "PUBLICATION_STATUS")
  arms << plant_arm("the page stops pointing at the section that states it",
                        planted.call("canonical section url", page,
                                     page.gsub(status_url, "the reference monorepo")),
                        sources, "PUBLICATION_STATUS")

# ── T-184's four plants ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE COORDINATES ARE READ OFF THE PAGE, never typed: a verb the page BOTH
# declares and routes, and the exact line it is routed with. A typed
# "catalog" would go on matching a page that had renamed the verb, and the
# arms would pass while aiming at nothing -- the failure this file's header
# records about hand-kept plant tables.
page_rts   = wire_routes(page_ruby(page)).select { |r| r[:verb] }
route_verb = (declared_verbs(page_ruby(page)) & page_rts.map { |r| r[:verb] }).first
if route_verb.nil?
  warn "self-test cannot derive its T-184 plants: the page declares no verb it also routes " \
       "(#{page_rts.size} route(s) drawn, #{declared_verbs(page_ruby(page)).size} verb(s) declared)"
  return 1
end
route_re = /^[ \t]*(?:get|post)[ \t]+"[^"]*"[ \t]*,[ \t]*to:[ \t]*"[^"]*"[^\n]*kiosk_verb:[ \t]*"#{Regexp.escape(route_verb)}"[^\n]*\n/
mount_re = /^[ \t]*mount[ \t]+Kiosk::Server::Engine[^\n]*\n/

# (a) THE SPLIT IS GONE -- the page stops reaching its wire file the way
# every origin does, so an operator copying it puts the wire back in
# config/routes.rb interleaved with their own pages.
arms << plant_arm("the page stops reaching the wire file with draw(:#{f[:draw]})",
                  planted.call("the draw split", page,
                               page.gsub("draw(:#{f[:draw]})", "draw(:wire)")),
                  sources, "ROUTE_FILE")
# (b) K-1394's own shape, one file over: a verb the page TEACHES and does not
# ROUTE. Before T-183 that was impossible -- the catch-all served it -- which
# is exactly why the page needs a rule for it now.
arms << plant_arm("#{route_verb} is declared on the page and never routed",
                  planted.call("the #{route_verb} route line", page, page.sub(route_re, "")),
                  sources, "VERB_ROUTES")
# (c) And the drift a well-meant edit makes: the METHOD stops following the
# KIND, so a query is taught as a POST (or an action as a GET).
arms << plant_arm("#{route_verb} routed with the other HTTP method",
                  planted.call("the #{route_verb} method", page,
                               page.sub(route_re) { |line|
                                 line.sub(/\A([ \t]*)(get|post)\b/) { "#{$1}#{$2 == "get" ? "post" : "get"}" }
                               }),
                  sources, "VERB_ROUTES")
# (d) THE MOUNT DEMOTED BELOW THE VERBS. Rails dispatches the first matching
# route, so this is the ordering property in its broken state: an operator
# verb drawn above the mount shadows the protocol plane, and the page would
# be teaching it.
demoted = lambda do
  text = page[mount_re]
  break page if text.nil?

  rest = page.sub(mount_re, "")
  last = rest.rindex(/^[ \t]*(?:get|post)[ \t]+"[^"]*"[ \t]*,[ \t]*to:[^\n]*\n/)
  break page if last.nil?

  eol = rest.index("\n", last)
  break page if eol.nil?

  rest[0..eol] + text + rest[(eol + 1)..]
end.call
arms << plant_arm("the mount drawn BELOW the page's verb routes",
                  planted.call("the mount's position", page, demoted),
                  sources, "MOUNT_FIRST")
# (e) THE CATCH-ALL BACK ON THE PAGE, in the exact spelling the engine drew
# through 0.4.12 and T-183 deleted.
arms << plant_arm("the :kiosk_verb catch-all taught in place of #{route_verb}'s line",
                  planted.call("the catch-all", page,
                               page.sub(route_re, %(get "/kiosk/:kiosk_verb", to: "kiosk/server/verb#show"\n))),
                  sources, "ROUTE_PATTERN")

  puts "── vacuity (derivation side blanked) ──"
  # The resolution K-1354 put in place of a printed note: rename the constant
  # where it is DEFINED, and the declaration citing it stops resolving. The rule
  # must SAY so and fail -- an unresolvable constant is precisely the state it
  # used to pass over in silence.
  const_short  = kw_const[3].split("::").last
  const_rename = lambda do |src|
    src.sub(/^(\s*)#{Regexp.escape(const_short)}(\s*=)/) { "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}SELF_TEST_RENAMED#{Regexp.last_match(2)}" }
  end
  const_sources = sources.merge(uuid_check: const_rename.call(sources[:uuid_check]),
                                wire_arguments: const_rename.call(sources[:wire_arguments]))
  planted.call("constant #{kw_const[3]} renamed at its definition",
               sources[:uuid_check] + sources[:wire_arguments],
               const_sources[:uuid_check] + const_sources[:wire_arguments])
  arms << vacuity_arm("#{kw_const[3]} no longer resolves to a literal",
                        page, const_sources, "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")
  # STATUS_PROSE (K-1519). Three arms, one per way the disclaimer clause can
  # stop being true without anyone touching the sentence: the vocabulary it
  # recognises drains, the page stops carrying the sub-class, and the guard it
  # names stops being there. The third is why `site_checkout` honours
  # KIOSK_TECH_DIR — a filesystem lookup nothing can plant into is a lookup
  # nobody has proved is happening.
  arms << vacuity_arm("the problem-schema mirror yields no code vocabulary", page,
                        sources.merge(problem_schema: "{}"), "STATUS_PROSE")
  arms << vacuity_arm("the page stops stating any status beside a code",
                        page.gsub(/\b[1-5]\d\d\b/, "HTTP"), sources, "STATUS_PROSE")
  empty_site = File.join(Dir.tmpdir, "onboarding-self-test-nosite-#{Process.pid}")
  FileUtils.mkdir_p(empty_site)
  begin
    prev_site = ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"]
    ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"] = empty_site
    arms << vacuity_arm("the sibling checkout has no bin/check-problem-pages", page, sources,
                          "STATUS_PROSE")
  ensure
    prev_site.nil? ? ENV.delete("KIOSK_TECH_DIR") : ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"] = prev_site
    FileUtils.rm_rf(empty_site)
  end

  arms << vacuity_arm("generator template loses its c.handlers example", page,
                        sources.merge(generator: sources[:generator].gsub(/c\.handlers\s*=\s*%w\[[^\]]*\]/, "c.handlers = []")), "HANDLER_NAMES")
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} loses required:", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].sub(req_lit, "")), "SLOTS_REQUIRED")
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's #{WORKED_VERB} is renamed away", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].sub(/^(\s*)def #{WORKED_VERB}$/, "\\1def #{WORKED_VERB}_renamed")), "SLOTS_PROPERTIES")
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery declares no integer input parameter", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].gsub('type: "integer"', 'type: "number"'),
                                      orders: sources[:orders].gsub('type: "integer"', 'type: "number"')), "INTEGER_COERCION")
  arms << vacuity_arm("the page loses every example_params",
                        page.gsub("example_params(", "example_args("), sources, "FROZEN_DATE")
  # Through DATE_CONSTRUCTOR itself, not through the literal "Date.parse": the
  # origin names more than one constructor and a literal gsub left one standing,
  # which turned this VACUITY arm into an inequality arm without saying so (K-1411).
  restub = ->(src) { src.gsub(DATE_CONSTRUCTOR) { "DateStub.#{Regexp.last_match(1)}" } }
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery stops parsing a date with a `Date.` constructor", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: restub.(sources[:storefront]),
                                      wire_arguments: restub.(sources[:wire_arguments])),
                        "DATE_PARSER")
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery registers exactly the page's classes", page,
                        sources.merge(getgrocery_init: sources[:getgrocery_init].sub(divergent, f[:handlers].first)), "GETGROCERY_HANDLERS")
  # REQUIRE_OPTIONAL's subject is «an argument some SHARED verb leaves
  # optional», so blanking one verb no longer empties it -- the ban reaches
  # every verb both sides declare. This makes EVERY verb of both controllers
  # require every parameter it declares, verb by verb, off its own derived
  # (required, properties) pair.
  require_everything = lambda do |src|
    declared_verbs(src).reduce(src) do |acc, verb|
      sch = verb_input_schema(src, verb)
      next acc if sch.nil? || sch[0].nil? || sch[1].nil? || sch[1].empty?

      acc.sub("required: [#{sch[0].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]",
              "required: [#{sch[1].map(&:inspect).join(", ")}]")
    end
  end
  arms << vacuity_arm("every getgrocery verb requires every parameter it declares", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: require_everything.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: require_everything.call(sources[:orders])), "REQUIRE_OPTIONAL")
  arms << vacuity_arm("the engine's gemspec stops naming Rails", page,
                        sources.merge(server_gemspec: sources[:server_gemspec].gsub(/add_dependency\s+"(?:#{RAILS_COMPONENTS.join("|")})"[^\n]*/, "# removed")), "RAILS_FLOOR")
  arms << vacuity_arm("kiosk-core stops declaring required_ruby_version", page,
                        sources.merge(core_gemspec: sources[:core_gemspec].gsub(/required_ruby_version[^\n]*/, "# removed")), "RUBY_FLOOR")
  arms << vacuity_arm("the README's canonical line loses its source qualifier", page,
                        sources.merge(readme: sources[:readme].gsub(/^gem\s+"kiosk-all"[^\n]*/, %(gem "kiosk-all"))), "INSTALL_SOURCE")
  # Both write-surface rules derive their SUBJECT from getgrocery, so blanking
  # that side must make them say so rather than pass. A renamed verb on the demo
  # side leaves nothing shared to compare.
  renamed = ->(src) { src.gsub(/^(\s*)def ([a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)$/) { "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}def #{Regexp.last_match(2)}_renamed" } }
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery declares no verb the page also declares", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: renamed.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: renamed.call(sources[:orders])), "WRITE_REQUIRED")
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery declares no array-of-objects parameter", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: sources[:storefront].gsub('type: "array"', 'type: "string"'),
                                      orders: sources[:orders].gsub('type: "array"', 'type: "string"')), "ITEM_SHAPE")
  arms << vacuity_arm("the engine's RESCUED_HINTS map is emptied", page,
                        sources.merge(server_errors: sources[:server_errors]
                          .sub(/RESCUED_HINTS\s*=\s*\{.*?\}\.freeze/m, "RESCUED_HINTS = {}.freeze")),
                        "ERROR_REDACTION")
  # Renaming the keywords rather than deleting them keeps the declarations
  # parseable, so the rule reaches its own vacuity test instead of failing
  # earlier for an unrelated reason.
  dekeyword = ->(src) { src.gsub(/\b(#{ASSERTION_KEYWORDS.join("|")}):/) { "x#{Regexp.last_match(1)}:" } }
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery asserts no JSON Schema keyword anywhere", page,
                        sources.merge(storefront: dekeyword.call(sources[:storefront]),
                                      orders: dekeyword.call(sources[:orders])), "PROPERTY_KEYWORDS")
  # R15's chain starts at the origin's summed total. Break the first link and the
  # whole ceiling is underivable -- the rule must SAY it holds nothing.
  no_sum = sources[:create_order_op].gsub(".sum", ".count")
  planted.call("getgrocery's create operation no longer sums", sources[:create_order_op], no_sum)
  arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's create operation sums no total", page,
                        sources.merge(create_order_op: no_sum), "TOTAL_GUARD")
  # R16's chain is rooted in the slot module's own clock reader. Rename it at
  # its DEFINITION and every guard that reads it still names it, so the chain
  # resolves to nothing -- which the rule must SAY rather than pass over.
  # `\b` and not `$`: the reader takes the zone it is being asked about since the
  # per-resource clock rule, so `def now` is followed by a parameter list. An
  # end-anchored pattern stopped matching and the plant became a NO-OP, which is
  # what {planted} exists to catch.
  no_clock = sources[:delivery_slots].sub(/^(\s*)def now\b/, "\\1def clock_renamed")
  planted.call("the slot module's clock reader renamed at its definition",
               sources[:delivery_slots], no_clock)
  arms << vacuity_arm("the slot module declares no clock reader", page,
                        sources.merge(delivery_slots: no_clock), "PAST_WINDOW")
  # R17 reads a **bolded** claim out of the README's install section. Strip the
  # emphasis and there is no canonical claim to copy -- red, naming itself,
  # rather than a page held to a sentence nobody states.
  no_claim = sources[:readme].gsub("**", "")
  planted.call("the README's bold emphasis removed", sources[:readme], no_claim)
  arms << vacuity_arm("the README states no bolded publication-status claim", page,
                        sources.merge(readme: no_claim), "PUBLICATION_STATUS")

# T-184's four, one per rule. Each blanks the getgrocery side the rule reads,
# so a rule that can no longer compute its own subject SAYS it holds nothing
# rather than passing over an empty comparison.
no_draw = sources[:app_routes].sub(/^([ \t]*)draw\(/) { "#{$1}nodraw(" }
planted.call("getgrocery's draw call renamed", sources[:app_routes], no_draw)
arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's config/routes.rb makes no draw call", page,
                    sources.merge(app_routes: no_draw), "ROUTE_FILE")
no_names = sources[:wire_file].gsub("kiosk_verb:", "other_key:")
planted.call("getgrocery's routes stop defaulting kiosk_verb", sources[:wire_file], no_names)
arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's wire file names no verb on any route", page,
                    sources.merge(wire_file: no_names), "VERB_ROUTES")
no_mount = sources[:wire_file].sub("mount Kiosk::Server::Engine", "mount Kiosk::Other::Engine")
planted.call("getgrocery's engine mount renamed", sources[:wire_file], no_mount)
arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's wire file mounts no Kiosk engine", page,
                    sources.merge(wire_file: no_mount), "MOUNT_FIRST")
no_routes = sources[:wire_file].gsub(/^(get|post)([ \t])/) { "x#{$1}#{$2}" }
planted.call("getgrocery's route lines disabled", sources[:wire_file], no_routes)
arms << vacuity_arm("getgrocery's wire file draws no route at all", page,
                    sources.merge(wire_file: no_routes), "ROUTE_PATTERN")

  # ── the network reader (K-1367) ────────────────────────────────────────────
  puts
  puts "── the --network reader, on the body Net::HTTP actually returns ──"
  binary = page.dup.force_encoding(Encoding::BINARY)
  got, provenance_net, reason_net = network_resolved(binary)
  decoded = !got.nil? && got.encoding == Encoding::UTF_8 && got.valid_encoding?
  clean   = false
  crash   = nil
  if decoded
    FAILURES.clear
    begin
      quiet { run_rules(got, sources) }
      clean = FAILURES.empty?
    rescue StandardError => e
      crash = "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
    end
  end
  arms << net_arm("an ASCII-8BIT body reaches every rule as text",
                      decoded && clean,
                      if !decoded
                        "!! resolve_page returned #{got.nil? ? reason_net.to_s : got.encoding.to_s}"
                      elsif crash
                        "!! #{RULES.size} rules did not run: #{crash}"
                      elsif !clean
                        "!! #{FAILURES.size} rule(s) red through the network reader that are green off disk"
                      else
                        "-> #{RULES.size} rules ran over #{provenance_net}"
                      end)
  # And the bytes are really COMPARED: an empty string would satisfy the arm
  # above and hold nothing.
  drifted_bytes = page.gsub("Rails #{f[:rails]}", "Rails 7.1")
  planted.call("Rails floor, through the network reader", page, drifted_bytes)
  got2, = network_resolved(drifted_bytes.dup.force_encoding(Encoding::BINARY))
  caught = false
  unless got2.nil?
    FAILURES.clear
    begin
      quiet { run_rules(got2, sources) }
      caught = FAILURES.any? { |x| x.start_with?("RAILS_FLOOR:") }
    rescue StandardError => e
      crash = "#{e.class}: #{e.message}"
    end
  end
  arms << net_arm("a break planted in those BYTES still goes red", caught,
                      caught ? "-> RAILS_FLOOR" : "!! RAILS_FLOOR stayed green#{crash ? " (#{crash})" : ""}")

  # ── THE PRECEDENCE ARMS (K-1388) ──────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # A DECOY ON DISK is the only honest way to ask this. `KIOSK_TECH_DIR` used to
  # be resolved BEFORE the network branch, so the flag handed back a local file
  # while the run line said it had read the published page — and it went
  # unnoticed precisely because the helper above unset the variable, which meant
  # no arm ever put the two in the same room. So the arm sets it, points it at a
  # page whose bytes could not possibly be the stub's, and asks what comes back.
  # Bytes rather than provenance: a run line can say anything.
  decoy_dir  = File.join(Dir.tmpdir, "onboarding-self-test-decoy-#{Process.pid}")
  decoy_body = "<html>DECOY: this file must never be what --network returns</html>\n"
  FileUtils.mkdir_p(decoy_dir)
  File.write(File.join(decoy_dir, PAGE_BASENAME), decoy_body)
  prev_env = ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"]
  begin
    ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"] = decoy_dir
    shadow_body, shadow_prov, shadow_reason = network_resolved(page.dup.force_encoding(Encoding::BINARY))
    served = !shadow_body.nil? && shadow_body.include?("Rails #{f[:rails]}") && !shadow_body.include?("DECOY")
    arms << net_arm("KIOSK_TECH_DIR does not shadow --network", served,
                    if shadow_body.nil?
                      "!! resolve_page returned no page at all: #{shadow_reason}"
                    elsif shadow_body.include?("DECOY")
                      "!! --network handed back the local checkout (#{shadow_prov})"
                    elsif !served
                      "!! --network returned bytes that are neither the stub nor the decoy"
                    else
                      "-> #{shadow_prov}"
                    end)

    # And the other half of the same rule: two EXPLICIT selectors naming two
    # sources is refused rather than silently resolved to whichever branch is
    # written first. This one needs no stub — the refusal happens before any
    # fetch — but it does need a file that exists, or the arm would pass on the
    # ordinary «--page does not exist» error instead.
    both_body, _both_prov, both_reason =
      resolve_page(explicit: File.join(decoy_dir, PAGE_BASENAME), network: true)
    refused = both_body.nil? && both_reason.to_s.include?("--network")
    arms << net_arm("--page WITH --network is refused, not silently preferred", refused,
                    refused ? "-> #{both_reason.to_s.split(" and ").first} and --network" \
                            : "!! resolve_page returned #{both_body.nil? ? both_reason.inspect : "a page"}")
  ensure
    prev_env.nil? ? ENV.delete("KIOSK_TECH_DIR") : ENV["KIOSK_TECH_DIR"] = prev_env
    FileUtils.rm_rf(decoy_dir)
  end

  FAILURES.clear
  puts
  puts "── unplanted ──"
  run_rules(page, sources)
  clean = FAILURES.empty?
  puts "  #{clean ? "ok" : "FAIL"}   #{real ? "the working tree" : "the built-in fixture"} holds all #{RULES.size} rules"

  bad = arms.count { |(_kind, ok)| !ok }
  # THE SPLIT IS COUNTED, NEVER SUBTRACTED (K-1389). Each arm above declared its
  # kind by the helper it was pushed through, so this is a tally of what actually
  # ran rather than arithmetic on two totals that assumed a shape the arms never
  # had. An arm carrying a kind this file does not know, or a declared kind with
  # no arm at all, FAILS the run: the first means a fourth helper was added
  # without teaching the summary, and the second means a whole section stopped
  # running while the remaining arms went on printing a clean verdict — which is
  # the vacuity this counting exists to make visible.
  tally   = arms.group_by(&:first).transform_values(&:size)
  unknown = tally.keys - ARM_KINDS
  empty   = ARM_KINDS - tally.keys
  split   = ARM_KINDS.map { |k| "#{tally.fetch(k, 0)} #{k}" }.join(", ")
  unless unknown.empty?
    puts "  FAIL  arm kind(s) #{unknown.inspect} are not in ARM_KINDS — teach the summary the new " \
         "helper, or push through one it knows."
  end
  unless empty.empty?
    puts "  FAIL  declared arm kind(s) #{empty.inspect} ran ZERO arms. A section that stopped " \
         "running leaves every remaining arm green over less than it says."
  end
  puts
  # Name the flag in the output. A run that never says it self-tested is no
  # evidence the flag did anything, because a script that IGNORES an
  # unrecognised flag and performs its ordinary run looks identical from
  # outside -- so the line below is the only thing distinguishing the two.
  ok = bad.zero? && clean && unknown.empty? && empty.empty?
  if ok
    puts "check-onboarding-derivation --self-test: OK — #{arms.size} arm(s) (#{split}) over " \
         "#{RULES.size} rules, every one caught."
  else
    puts "check-onboarding-derivation --self-test: FAIL — #{arms.size} arm(s) (#{split}), " \
         "#{bad} did not go red; unplanted run #{clean ? "clean" : "RED"}."
  end
  ok ? 0 : 1
end

# ── main ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

network  = ARGV.delete("--network")
list     = ARGV.delete("--list")
selftest = ARGV.delete("--self-test")
explicit = nil
if (i = ARGV.index("--page"))
  explicit = ARGV[i + 1]
  ARGV.slice!(i, 2)
end
unless ARGV.empty?
  warn "unknown argument(s): #{ARGV.join(" ")}"
  exit 2
end

exit run_list if list
exit run_self_test if selftest

page, provenance, reason = resolve_page(explicit: explicit, network: !network.nil?)
if page.nil?
  warn "check-onboarding-derivation: #{reason}"
  exit 1
end

puts "onboarding.html read from #{provenance}"
puts "derivations read from this checkout (#{ROOT})"
puts
sources = load_sources
run_rules(page, sources)
puts
if FAILURES.empty?
  puts "#{RULES.size} rules, all held."
  exit 0
end
puts "#{FAILURES.size} of #{RULES.size} rules FAILED:"
FAILURES.each { |f| puts "  - #{f}" }
exit 1
